        [Turn 5 REASONING] (2755 chars)
        \[
p<q<r\qquad p,q,r\ \text{primes}
\]

Assume that  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A&=p+q-r,\\
B&=q+r-p,\\
C&=r+p-q,\\
D&=p+q+r
\end{aligned}
\]

are all prime numbers and that one of the three sums of two of the
primes equals \(800\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Which two‑prime sum can be \(800\)?

If \(q+r=800\) then \(r<800\) (because \(q>p\)).  
If \(r+p=800\) the same conclusion holds.  
If \(p+q=800\) we also have \(r<p+q\), so again \(r<800\).

Hence in every case  

\[
r<800 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Reduce to the case \(p+q=800\)

Let us treat the three possibilities separately.

*If \(q+r=800\):* then \(p+q-r = p-(800-q) = p+q-800\) would be negative,
contradicting that it is a prime.  
*If \(r+p=800\):* similarly \(q+r-p = q-(800-p)=q+p-800<0\).

Thus the only possible case is  

\[
\boxed{p+q=800 } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Consequences of \(p+q=800\)

Write  

\[
q=800-p .
\]

Then  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A &=p+q-r = 800-r,\\[2mm]
B &=q+r-p = 800+r-2p,\\[2mm]
C &=r+p-q = r+2p-800,\\[2mm]
D &=p+q+r = 800+r .
\end{aligned}
\]

All four numbers must be prime, and of course \(p,q,r\) are prime.
Set  

\[
x = 2p-800\qquad(\text{so }x\text{ is even and }-800<x<800).
\]

