--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Parity

All three numbers \(p,q,r\) are odd.  
Indeed, if one of them were \(2\) then a sum containing it would be odd,
but the only sum that may equal \(800\) is even; hence the two numbers in
that sum are odd, and the third one must also be odd.

Consequently  

* \(p+q,\;q+r,\;r+p\) are even,
* \(p+q-r,\;q+r-p,\;r+p-q,\;p+q+r\) are odd.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Which sum can be \(800\)?

If \(q+r=800\) then \(r=800-q<800\) and \(p<q<r\) forces \(p\le 2\).
With \(p=2\) we would have \(p+q-r=2+q-r<0\), impossible.
The same argument rules out \(p+r=800\).

Hence the only possible case is  

\[
\boxed{p+q=800 } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Reformulating the conditions

Write \(q=800-p\;(p<400)\).  
The four required primes become  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A &= p+q-r = 800-r,\\[2pt]
B &= q+r-p = 800+r-2p,\\[2pt]
C &= r+p-q = r+2p-800,\\[2pt]
D &= p+q+r = 800+r .
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus we need primes  

\[
p,\;800-p,\;r,\;800-r,\;800+r,\;800+r-2p,\;r+2p-800 .
\tag{1}
\]

Because \(r>q\) we have \(r>400\); consequently \(800-r<400\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Searching for the largest possible \(r\)

From (1) the numbers \(r\) and \(800-r\) must both be prime; i.e. \(r\) is a
prime belonging to a Goldbach pair of \(800\).
The largest prime below \(800\) that forms such a pair is  

\[
r=797,\qquad 800-r=3 .
\]

Now we must find a prime \(p<400\) such that the remaining three numbers in
(1) are also prime.  Substituting \(r=797\) gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
800+r   &= 1597,\\
800+r-2p&= 1597-2p,\\
r+2p-800&= 2p-3 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence we need \(p\) prime with  

\[
p,\;800-p,\;1597,\;1597-2p,\;2p-3\ \text{ all prime}.
\tag{2}
\]

Checking the primes \(p<400\) that satisfy (2) yields exactly four
possibilities:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
p & q=800-p & 2p-3 \\ \hline
13  & 787 & 23\\
43  & 757 & 83\\
157 & 643 & 311\\
223 & 577 & 443
\end{array}
\]

In each case the numbers listed are prime, and together with
\(r=797\) they satisfy all original conditions.

Since any larger prime \(r\) would have to be \(\ge 799\), which is not
prime, \(r=797\) is the maximal possible value.

