        [Turn 18 REASONING] (3308 chars)
        \[
3(a_i^2+a_j^2)+15a_i^2a_j^2\ge (4a_ia_j+1)^2\qquad (1)
\]

For a pair \((x,y)\;(x,y>0)\) put  

\[
F(x,y)=3x^{2}+3y^{2}-x^{2}y^{2}-8xy-1 .
\]

Then \((1)\) is exactly \(F(x,y)\ge 0\).
For a fixed \(x\) the inequality is a quadratic in \(y\):

\[
(3-x^{2})y^{2}-8xy+(3x^{2}-1)\ge 0 .\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The equality curve  

If the discriminant of (2) is zero we obtain the equality case.
The discriminant is  

\[
\Delta =64x^{2}-4(3-x^{2})(3x^{2}-1)=12(x^{2}+1)^{2}>0 .
\]

Hence (2) has two real roots

\[
y_{1,2}(x)=\frac{4x\pm\sqrt 3\,(x^{2}+1)}{\,3-x^{2}\,}. \tag{3}
\]

Both roots are positive whenever \(0<x<\sqrt3\); for \(x>\sqrt3\) only the
“minus’’ sign gives a positive root.
Denote  

\[
T(x)=\frac{4x+\sqrt 3\,(x^{2}+1)}{3-x^{2}},\qquad
G(x)=\frac{4x-\sqrt 3\,(x^{2}+1)}{3-x^{2}} .
\]

For \(0<x<\sqrt3\) we have  

\[
0<G(x)<x<T(x),\qquad T(G(x))=x,\;G(T(x))=x .\tag{4}
\]

Thus the equality \(F(x,y)=0\) holds **iff** \(y=T(x)\) or \(y=G(x)\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Structure of a set satisfying (1)

Let \(\mathcal S=\{a_{1},\dots ,a_{n}\}\) be a set of distinct positive
numbers such that (1) holds for every pair.
Take any element \(a\in\mathcal S\).

*If \(a<\sqrt3\).*  
Then the only positive numbers that give equality with \(a\) are
\(T(a)\) and \(G(a)\) (by (3)).  
If both of them belong to \(\mathcal S\) we already have the three
different numbers  

\[
G(a)<a<T(a). \tag{5}
\]

For the pair \((G(a),T(a))\) we have  

\[
F\bigl(G(a),T(a)\bigr)=
F\bigl(G(a),a\bigr)+F\bigl(a,T(a)\bigr)>0,
\]

so the inequality is strict for this pair, while it is an equality for
the two adjacent pairs.  
Applying \(T\) or \(G\) once more brings us back to a number already
present in (5); consequently no further distinct element can be added
to the set.

*If \(a>\sqrt3\).*  
Then the only positive root of (2) is \(G(a)\ (<\sqrt3)\); the other root
is negative.  Hence any element larger than \(\sqrt3\) can be paired
with at most one element smaller than \(\sqrt3\), namely \(G(a)\).  By the
previous paragraph the numbers \(\{G(a),a\}\) can be extended by at most
one more element, namely \(T\bigl(G(a)\bigr)\).  Again we obtain at most
three distinct numbers.

Therefore **any** set of distinct positive reals satisfying (1) contains
at most three elements.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Existence of a set with three elements  

Choose a number \(x\) with \(0<x<\sqrt3\) (for instance \(x=\frac12\)).
Define  

\[
y=G(x)=\frac{4x-\sqrt3\,(x^{2}+1)}{3-x^{2}},\qquad
z=T(x)=\frac{4x+\sqrt3\,(x^{2}+1)}{3-x^{2}} .
\]

Then \(0<y<x<z\) and, by construction,

\[
F(y,x)=F(x,z)=0,\qquad F(y,z)>0 .
\]

Hence the three numbers \(y,x,z\) satisfy (1) for every pair.
For example, with \(x=\frac12\) we obtain  

\[
\bigl\{\,0.5,\;1.5145685489,\;16.6602540378\,\bigr\},
\]

which indeed fulfills the required inequality.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Largest possible \(n\)

We have exhibited a set of three distinct positive numbers satisfying
the condition, and we have proved that a set with four distinct numbers
cannot exist. Consequently

\[
\boxed{\,n_{\max}=3\,}.
\]

