        \[
3(a_i^{2}+a_j^{2})+15a_i^{2}a_j^{2}\ge (4a_i a_j+1)^{2}
\qquad (1)
\]

Let  

\[
F(x,y)=3(x^{2}+y^{2})-x^{2}y^{2}-8xy-1 .
\]

The inequality \((1)\) is equivalent to \(F(a_i ,a_j)\ge 0\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The admissible region for a pair  

For a fixed positive \(x\) write the inequality as a quadratic in \(y\):

\[
(3-x^{2})y^{2}-8xy+(3x^{2}-1)\ge 0 .
\tag{2}
\]

The discriminant of (2) is  

\[
\Delta =64x^{2}-4(3-x^{2})(3x^{2}-1)=12(x^{2}+1)^{2}>0 .
\]

Hence (2) has the two real roots  

\[
y_{1,2}(x)=\frac{4x\pm\sqrt{3}\,(x^{2}+1)}{3-x^{2}} .
\tag{3}
\]

* If \(0<x<\sqrt3\) then \(3-x^{2}>0\); the quadratic opens upward and
\(F(x,y)\ge0\) holds for  

\[
y\le y_{1}(x)\qquad\text{or}\qquad y\ge y_{2}(x).
\tag{4}
\]

* If \(x>\sqrt3\) then \(3-x^{2}<0\); the quadratic opens downward and
\(F(x,y)\ge0\) holds for  

\[
y_{2}(x)\le y\le y_{1}(x),
\]

which for positive \(y\) reduces to  

\[
0<y\le y_{1}(x). \tag{5}
\]

A direct computation gives  

\[
y_{1}\!\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt3}\right)=\frac{1}{\sqrt3},
\qquad
y_{2}\!\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt3}\right)=\sqrt3 .
\tag{6}
\]

Consequently

* two numbers **both** \(\le \dfrac1{\sqrt3}\) cannot satisfy (1);
* two numbers **both** \(\ge\sqrt3\) cannot satisfy (1);
* two numbers **both** in the open interval \(\bigl(\dfrac1{\sqrt3},\sqrt3\bigr)\) cannot satisfy (1).

Thus at most one element can lie in each of the three intervals  

\[
\boxed{I_{1}=(0,\tfrac1{\sqrt3}],\; I_{2}=(\tfrac1{\sqrt3},\sqrt3),\;
I_{3}=[\sqrt3,\infty)} .
\tag{7}
\]

Hence any admissible set contains at most three distinct numbers, i.e.  

\[
n\le 3 .
\tag{8}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Existence of a set with three elements  

Take  

\[
a_{1}= \frac14\;( \in I_{1}),\qquad
a_{2}= 1\;( \in I_{2}),\qquad
a_{3}= 4\;( \in I_{3}).
\]

We check the three pairs.

*For \((a_{1},a_{2})\):*  
\(a_{2}=1\ge y_{2}(a_{1})\) because \(y_{2}\!\left(\frac14\right)=
\frac{4\cdot\frac14+\sqrt3\bigl(\frac1{16}+1\bigr)}{3-\frac1{16}}
\approx 0.967<1\). Hence (4) is satisfied.

*For \((a_{1},a_{3})\):*  
\(a_{3}=4\ge y_{2}(a_{1})\) as above, so (4) holds.

*For \((a_{2},a_{3})\):*  
Since \(a_{3}> \sqrt3\), we must use (5).  
\(a_{2}=1\le y_{1}(a_{3})\) where  

\[
y_{1}(4)=\frac{4\cdot4-\sqrt3\,(4^{2}+1)}{3-4^{2}}
      =\frac{16- \sqrt3\cdot17}{-13}\approx 1.047>1 .
\]

Thus (5) is satisfied.

All three inequalities are strict, so the three numbers indeed satisfy (1). Therefore a set of three distinct positive reals exists.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Largest possible \(n\)

From (8) we have \(n\le 3\), and the construction above shows that
\(n=3\) is attainable.

\[
\boxed{\,n_{\max}=3\,}
\]

