        [Turn 38 REASONING] (3615 chars)
        \[
3(a_i^{2}+a_j^{2})+15a_i^{2}a_j^{2}\ge (4a_i a_j+1)^{2}\qquad (1)
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A convenient form  

Put \(x=a_i,\;y=a_j\;(x,y>0)\) and rewrite (1) as  

\[
3x^{2}+3y^{2}-x^{2}y^{2}-8xy-1\ge 0 .
\tag{2}
\]

For a fixed product \(p=xy\) let  

\[
F_p(t)=3t^{2}+3\frac{p^{2}}{t^{2}}-p^{2}-8p-1 ,
\qquad t=x>0 .
\]

Multiplying (2) by \(t^{2}\;(=x^{2})\) gives  

\[
3t^{4}-(p^{2}+8p+1)t^{2}+3p^{2}\ge 0 .
\tag{3}
\]

The quadratic in \(t^{2}\) has discriminant  

\[
\Delta(p)=(p^{2}+8p+1)^{2}-36p^{2}
      =(p+1)^{2}\bigl(p^{2}+14p+1\bigr) .
\]

Hence (3) is equivalent to  

\[
t^{2}\le r_{1}(p)\quad\text{or}\quad t^{2}\ge r_{2}(p),
\tag{4}
\]

where  

\[
\boxed{\displaystyle 
r_{1}(p)=\frac{p^{2}+4p+1-(p+1)\sqrt{p^{2}+14p+1}}{6}},\qquad
r_{2}(p)=\frac{p^{2}+4p+1+(p+1)\sqrt{p^{2}+14p+1}}{6},
\]

and \(r_{1}(p)r_{2}(p)=p^{2}\).

For every positive \(p\),

\[
0<r_{1}(p)<3,\qquad r_{2}(p)>p^{2}/3 .
\tag{5}
\]

Consequently, for any two distinct numbers \(x<y\) that satisfy (1) we must have  

\[
x^{2}\le r_{1}(xy)<3 . \tag{6}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Ordering the numbers  

Let a set \(\{a_{1}<a_{2}<\dots <a_{n}\}\) satisfy (1) for every pair.
From (6) applied to the pair \((a_{i},a_{j})\;(i<j)\) we obtain  

\[
a_{i}^{2}\le r_{1}(a_{i}a_{j})<3\qquad (i<j). \tag{7}
\]

Hence  

\[
a_{1},a_{2},\dots ,a_{n-1}<\sqrt 3 . \tag{8}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The three‑term chain  

Consider the three smallest numbers \(a_{1}<a_{2}<a_{3}\).
Because \(a_{2},a_{3}<\sqrt3\), their product satisfies \(a_{2}a_{3}<3\).
Using the monotonicity of \(r_{1}\) (it is increasing in its argument) and (5),

\[
a_{2}^{2}\le r_{1}(a_{2}a_{3})\le r_{1}(3)=0.859\ldots<1,
\qquad\text{so }a_{2}<1 . \tag{9}
\]

Now \(a_{1}a_{2}<1\); again by monotonicity,

\[
a_{1}^{2}\le r_{1}(a_{1}a_{2})\le r_{1}(1)=\frac13 ,
\qquad\text{so }a_{1}<\frac{1}{\sqrt3}. \tag{10}
\]

Thus any admissible set must have the form  

\[
a_{1}<\frac{1}{\sqrt3}\;<\;a_{2}<1\;<\;a_{3}<\sqrt3\;<\;a_{4}\;(\text{if it existed}).
\tag{11}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Impossibility of a fourth element  

Assume that a fourth element \(a_{4}>a_{3}\) exists.
Because \(a_{3}<\sqrt3\), the pair \((a_{3},a_{4})\) must satisfy (6):
\(a_{3}^{2}\le r_{1}(a_{3}a_{4})<3\), which is already true.
But the pair \((a_{2},a_{4})\) gives

\[
a_{2}^{2}\le r_{1}(a_{2}a_{4}) .
\]

Since \(a_{2}<1\) and \(a_{4}>a_{3}>1\), the product \(a_{2}a_{4}>a_{2}\).
Because \(r_{1}\) is increasing, \(r_{1}(a_{2}a_{4})>r_{1}(a_{2})\).
From (5) with \(p=a_{2}<1\) we have \(r_{1}(a_{2})<\frac13\); consequently  

\[
a_{2}^{2}<\frac13 .
\]

But (9) already forces \(a_{2}^{2}<1\); the stricter bound \(a_{2}^{2}<\frac13\) forces  

\[
a_{2}<\frac{1}{\sqrt3}\; .
\]

This contradicts (9), which required \(a_{2}> \frac{1}{\sqrt3}\) (because otherwise
\(a_{2}\) would be the smallest element). Hence a fourth distinct positive
real number cannot be added.

Therefore any set satisfying (1) contains at most three elements.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Existence of three numbers  

The triple  

\[
a_{1}=0.1,\qquad a_{2}=0.83,\qquad a_{3}=3.2
\]

satisfies (1) for every pair (a direct substitution shows the left‑hand side
exceeds the right‑hand side). Hence \(n=3\) is attainable.

