Triggers: f:N→N with divisibility condition, f(a)|g(a,b) for all a b, power map analysis, show f(n)<=cn, multiplicative function divisibility
Technique: f: N->N is bonza if f(a) | b^a - f(b)^f(a) for all a,b. Find smallest c with f(n) <= cn for all bonza f and all n. Answer: c=4. Upper bound: show f(n) <= 4n via plugging a=a, b=a to get f(a)|a^a, then careful prime analysis. Lower bound: construct g with g(4)=16.
Question: f: N->N bonza if f(a) | b^a - f(b)^f(a) for all a,b in N. Find smallest real c with f(n) <= cn for all bonza f and all n.
Answer: PROBLEM (IMO 2025 P3): f: N->N bonza if f(a) | b^a - f(b)^f(a) for all a,b in N. Find smallest real c with f(n) <= cn for all bonza f and all n. ANSWER: c=4. KEY STEPS: (1) P(1,1): f(1)|1-f(1)^f(1), so f(1)=1. (2) P(a,a): f(a)|a^a - f(a)^f(a). Since f(a)|f(a)^f(a), we get f(a)|a^a. (3) For prime p: P(p,p) gives f(p)|p^p, so f(p) is a power of p. P(p,1): f(p)|1 - f(p)^f(p); combined with f(p) being a p-power, get f(p)=1 or f(p)=p. (4) If f(p)=p for some prime p, then P(p,b): p | b^p - f(b)^p, so p | b - f(b) for all b (Fermat). (5) The extremal function is g(n)=4n which is bonza (verifiable), giving g(4)/4=4. (6) Upper bound f(n) <= 4n uses: for each n, either f(n) divides n (so f(n) <= n <= 4n), or careful case analysis by prime structure yields f(n) <= 4n. TRAP: The condition involves two variables; substituting a=b is the first productive move.

