        \[
a_1=1,\qquad a_n\mid a_k+a_{k+1}+\dots +a_{k+n-1}\ \ \forall k,n\in\mathbb N .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Consequences of the condition  

For fixed \(n\) and any \(k\),

\[
a_n\mid (a_k+\dots +a_{k+n-1})-(a_{k+1}+\dots +a_{k+n})
      =a_k-a_{k+n}.
\]

Hence  

\[
\boxed{a_{k+n}\equiv a_k\pmod{a_n}}\qquad\forall k,n .
\tag{1}
\]

Taking \(k=1\) we obtain  

\[
a_{n+1}\equiv a_1\equiv 1\pmod{a_n}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad 
a_{n+1}=1\ \text{or}\ a_{n+1}=1+ t\,a_n .
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A convenient extremal construction  

Put  

\[
a_{2m}=2^{\,m}-1,\qquad a_{2m+1}=1\qquad(m\ge 1).
\tag{3}
\]

*Odd indices.*  Since \(a_{2m+1}=1\), condition (1) is automatically satisfied.

*Even indices.*  
Consider any block of \(2m\) consecutive terms.  
Exactly \(m\) of them are even‑indexed and equal to  

\[
2^{p}-1,\,2^{p+1}-1,\dots ,2^{p+m-1}-1
\]

for some integer \(p\ge 1\); the other \(m\) terms are the odd ones and equal to \(1\).
Hence the sum of the block is  

\[
\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\bigl(2^{p+j}-1\bigr)+m
   =2^{p}\bigl(2^{m}-1\bigr).
\]

Because \(a_{2m}=2^{m}-1\), this sum is a multiple of \(a_{2m}\).  
Thus (3) satisfies the required divisibility for every \(k,n\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Maximality of the even terms  

Let \(n=2m\).  
From (1) with \(k=1\) we have \(a_{2m}\mid a_{2m+1}-1\).  
Since \(a_{2m+1}=1\) (otherwise the sum of the first \(2m\) terms would be larger), we obtain  

\[
a_{2m}\mid (a_1+\dots +a_{2m})=m\cdot1+\sum_{j=1}^{m}(2^{j}-1)
      =2^{m+1}-2 =2\bigl(2^{m}-1\bigr).
\]

The number \(a_{2m}\) is odd (it divides an odd number), therefore  

\[
a_{2m}\mid 2^{m}-1 .
\]

Consequently the largest possible value of \(a_{2m}\) is exactly \(2^{m}-1\), attained by the construction (3).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The maximal value of \(a_{5652}\)

\(5652\) is even, so write \(5652=2\cdot 2826\).  
The maximal possible value is  

\[
N_{\max}=2^{2826}-1 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Computing \(N_{\max}\pmod{776}\)

\[
N_{\max}\equiv 2^{2826}-1 \pmod{776}.
\]

Using modular exponentiation,

\[
2^{2826}\equiv 144\pmod{776}\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
2^{2826}-1\equiv 143\pmod{776}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,N\equiv 143\pmod{776}\,}
\]

