        [Turn 7 REASONING] (2793 chars)
        \[
\begin{aligned}
&x_{1},\; \frac1{x_{1}}+x_{2},\; \frac1{x_{2}}+x_{3},\dots ,
\frac1{x_{2024}}+x_{2025},\; \frac1{x_{2025}}+1
\end{aligned}
\]

Let  

\[
M=\min_{x_{1},\dots ,x_{2025}>0}\;
\max\Bigl\{x_{1},\frac1{x_{1}}+x_{2},\dots ,\frac1{x_{2025}}+1\Bigr\}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A necessary condition for \(M\)

If the maximum does not exceed a number \(t\,(>0)\) then we have  

\[
\begin{cases}
x_{1}\le t,\\[2mm]
\displaystyle \frac1{x_{i}}+x_{i+1}\le t\qquad(i=1,\dots ,2024),\\[2mm]
\displaystyle \frac1{x_{2025}}+1\le t .
\end{cases}\tag{1}
\]

From the second line of (1)

\[
x_{i+1}\le t-\frac1{x_{i}}\qquad(i\ge 1).
\]

Because the right–hand side is increasing in \(x_{i}\;(x_{i}>0)\), the
largest possible value of each \(x_{i}\) is obtained when all the
inequalities in (1) are equalities.  
Hence, if a number \(t\) is admissible, the sequence

\[
\boxed{\;x_{1}=t,\qquad x_{i+1}=t-\frac1{x_{i}}\;(i=1,\dots ,2024)\;}
\tag{2}
\]

must satisfy the last inequality of (1), i.e.

\[
\frac1{x_{2025}}+1\le t\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
x_{2025}\ge\frac1{t-1}. \tag{3}
\]

Denote by \(f_{t}^{(k)}(t)\) the \(k\)-fold iteration of the map
\(f_{t}(x)=t-\dfrac1x\) starting from \(t\).  Then \(x_{2025}=f_{t}^{(2024)}(t)\).
Condition (3) becomes  

\[
f_{t}^{(2024)}(t)\ge\frac1{t-1}. \tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Solving the condition

The iteration (2) converges (for any starting value) to the larger root
of the fixed‑point equation  

\[
x=t-\frac1x\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x^{2}-t x+1=0 .
\]

Thus, for every \(t>0\),

\[
\lim_{k\to\infty}f_{t}^{(k)}(t)=\frac{t+\sqrt{t^{2}-4}}{2}.
\]

For the right–hand side of (4) to be real we must have \(t^{2}-4\ge0\);
hence

\[
\boxed{t\ge 2}.
\]

If \(t=2\) the iteration gives the explicit sequence  

\[
x_{1}=2,\;x_{2}=2-\frac12= \frac32,\;
x_{3}=2-\frac{2}{3}= \frac{4}{3},\;
x_{4}=2-\frac{3}{4}= \frac{5}{4},\dots ,
\]

which is decreasing and tends to \(1\).  In particular  

\[
x_{2025}=2-\frac1{x_{2024}}>\frac1{2-1}=1,
\]

so (3) is satisfied with equality in the limit.  Consequently the
maximum of the 2026 numbers equals \(2\).  Therefore

\[
\boxed{M=2}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The required polynomial

The problem asks for the smallest degree of an integer‑coefficient
polynomial \(F(x)\) that is divisible by  

\[
x^{2}-Mx+1=x^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.
\]

Since the divisor already has integer coefficients, the smallest
possible degree of such a polynomial is the degree of the divisor
itself, namely \(2\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\text{The minimal degree of }F(x)\text{ is }2.}
\]

