        \[
x_{1},x_{2},\dots ,x_{2025}>0 ,\qquad 
\max\Bigl\{x_{1},\frac1{x_{1}}+x_{2},\dots ,\frac1{x_{2024}}+x_{2025},
\frac1{x_{2025}}+1\Bigr\}=M .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Determination of \(M\)

For a given number \(t>0\) the condition  

\[
\max\{x_{1},\frac1{x_{1}}+x_{2},\dots ,\frac1{x_{2024}}+x_{2025},
\frac1{x_{2025}}+1\}\le t
\]

is equivalent to the system  

\[
\begin{cases}
x_{1}\le t,\\[2mm]
x_{k+1}\le t-\dfrac1{x_{k}}\qquad(k=1,\dots ,2024),\\[2mm]
\frac1{x_{2025}}+1\le t .
\end{cases}
\tag{1}
\]

If a feasible sequence exists, we may increase each \(x_{k}\) until the
corresponding inequality becomes an equality; the maximal value does
not increase.  
Hence at the optimum all the inequalities in (1) are equalities:

\[
\boxed{\;
\begin{aligned}
x_{1}&=t,\\
x_{k+1}&=t-\frac1{x_{k}}\qquad(k=1,\dots ,2024),\\
\frac1{x_{2025}}+1&=t .
\end{aligned}}
\tag{2}
\]

Put \(\theta\) by  

\[
t=2\cos\theta ,\qquad 0<\theta<\frac{\pi}{2}.
\]

Then (2) gives  

\[
x_{k+1}=2\cos\theta-\frac1{x_{k}}
      =\frac{e^{i\theta}+e^{-i\theta}}{1}
        -\frac1{x_{k}}
      =e^{i\theta}\Bigl(\frac1{e^{i\theta}}- \frac1{x_{k}}\Bigr)
\]

and a short induction shows  

\[
x_{k}=2\cos(k\theta),\qquad k=1,2,\dots ,2025 .
\]

The last equality in (2) becomes  

\[
\frac1{2\cos(2025\theta)}+1=2\cos\theta .
\]

Using the identity \(\cos(2025\theta)=\cos\bigl((2025+1)\theta-\theta\bigr)
      =\cos\bigl((2026)\theta-\theta\bigr)\) we obtain  

\[
2\cos\theta=2\cos\bigl((2026)\theta\bigr).
\]

Since \(0<\theta<\pi/2\), the smallest positive solution is  

\[
(2026)\theta=\pi\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad 
\theta=\frac{\pi}{2026}= \frac{\pi}{2\cdot2025+3}.
\]

Consequently  

\[
\boxed{M=2\cos\frac{\pi}{2\cdot2025+3}=2\cos\frac{\pi}{4053}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A polynomial divisible by \(x^{2}-Mx+1\)

Write  

\[
M=2\cos\frac{\pi}{4053}= \zeta+\zeta^{-1},
\qquad 
\zeta=e^{i\pi/4053}.
\]

Hence  

\[
x^{2}-Mx+1=(x-\zeta)(x-\zeta^{-1}).
\]

The numbers \(\zeta\) and \(\zeta^{-1}\) are primitive
\(2\cdot4053\)-th roots of unity; i.e. they are among the roots of the
cyclotomic polynomial  

\[
\Phi_{2\cdot4053}(x)=\Phi_{4053}(x).
\]

The cyclotomic polynomial \(\Phi_{4053}(x)\) has integer coefficients,
is irreducible over \(\mathbb{Q}\), and its degree equals Euler’s totient
function  

\[
\deg\Phi_{4053}= \varphi(4053).
\]

Factorising \(4053\),

\[
4053 = 3\cdot7\cdot193,
\qquad 
\varphi(4053)=4053\Bigl(1-\frac13\Bigr)\Bigl(1-\frac17\Bigr)
               \Bigl(1-\frac1{193}\Bigr)=2304 .
\]

Because \(\Phi_{4053}(x)\) is the minimal polynomial over \(\mathbb{Q}\)
that contains the factor \((x-\zeta)(x-\zeta^{-1})\), any polynomial with
integer coefficients that is divisible by \(x^{2}-Mx+1\) must be a
multiple of \(\Phi_{4053}(x)\).  Therefore the smallest possible degree
of such a polynomial is exactly \(\deg\Phi_{4053}=2304\).

