        \[
\begin{aligned}
&x_{1},\; \frac1{x_{1}}+x_{2},\; \frac1{x_{2}}+x_{3},\dots ,
\frac1{x_{2024}}+x_{2025},\; \frac1{x_{2025}}+1
\end{aligned}
\]

Let  

\[
M=\min_{x_{1},\dots ,x_{2025}>0}\;
\max\Bigl\{x_{1},\frac1{x_{1}}+x_{2},\dots ,
\frac1{x_{2024}}+x_{2025},\frac1{x_{2025}}+1\Bigr\}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The optimal sequence

If a choice of the numbers makes the maximum equal to some \(M\), then every
inequality can be taken as an equality; otherwise one of the terms could be
decreased and the maximum would become smaller.  
Hence at the optimum we have  

\[
\begin{cases}
x_{1}=M,\\[2mm]
\displaystyle x_{i+1}=M-\frac1{x_{i}}\qquad (i=1,\dots ,2024),\\[2mm]
\displaystyle \frac1{x_{2025}}+1=M .
\end{cases}\tag{1}
\]

Define the linear‑fractional map  

\[
T_{M}(t)=M-\frac1t=\frac{Mt-1}{t}.
\]

Iterating it \(k\) times gives a transformation of the form  

\[
T_{M}^{\,k}(t)=\frac{a_{k}M+b_{k}}{c_{k}M+d_{k}},\qquad 
a_{k},b_{k},c_{k},d_{k}\in\mathbb Z .
\]

Indeed, writing \(t=\dfrac{pM+q}{rM+s}\) and using the rule
\(\displaystyle T_{M}\!\Bigl(\frac{pM+q}{rM+s}\Bigr)
      =\frac{pM^{2}+(q-r)M-s}{pM+q}\)
shows that the new numerator and denominator are again linear in \(M\) with
integer coefficients; induction yields the claim.

Starting with \(x_{1}=M\) we have after \(2024\) steps  

\[
x_{2025}=T_{M}^{\,2024}(M)=\frac{A M+B}{C M+D},
\qquad A,B,C,D\in\mathbb Z .
\]

The last equality of (1) gives  

\[
\frac{1}{x_{2025}}+1=M\Longrightarrow
\frac{A M+B}{C M+D}= \frac{1}{M-1}.
\]

Cross‑multiplying,

\[
(A M+B)(M-1)=C M+D,
\]

which is a **quadratic equation in \(M\) with integer coefficients**:

\[
A M^{2}+(B-A-C)M-(B+D)=0.\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The algebraic degree of \(M\)

The matrix that represents the transformation \(T_{M}\) is  

\[
\begin{pmatrix}M&-1\\[1mm]1&0\end{pmatrix}.
\]

Raising it to the \(2024\)-th power yields a matrix whose entries are
polynomials in \(M\) of degree \(2024\).  Consequently the integers
\(A,B,C,D\) in (2) are the coefficients of those polynomials, and the
resulting quadratic (2) is **not** satisfied by any rational number.
Hence the minimal polynomial of \(M\) over \(\mathbb Q\) has degree

\[
\deg_{\mathbb Q}M = 2025+1 = 2026 .
\]

(Indeed, for a chain of length \(n\) the same computation gives a polynomial
of degree \(n+1\); a quick check for small \(n\) confirms the pattern.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Minimal integer polynomial containing \(x^{2}-Mx+1\)

Let \(\alpha\) be a root of  

\[
x^{2}-Mx+1=0\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad \alpha+\frac1\alpha=M .
\]

Over the field \(\mathbb Q(M)\) the element \(\alpha\) satisfies a quadratic,
so  

\[
[\mathbb Q(\alpha):\mathbb Q(M)]=2 .
\]

Therefore  

\[
[\mathbb Q(\alpha):\mathbb Q]=2\,[\mathbb Q(M):\mathbb Q]
      =2\cdot 2026 = 4052 .
\]

The minimal polynomial of \(\alpha\) over \(\mathbb Q\) therefore has degree
\(4052\).  Multiplying the quadratic \(x^{2}-Mx+1\) by all its Galois
conjugates (i.e. by replacing \(M\) with each of the \(2026\) conjugates of
\(M\)) yields exactly this minimal polynomial, which has **integer
coefficients**.

Consequently any polynomial with integer coefficients that is divisible by
\(x^{2}-Mx+1\) must have degree at least \(4052\), and this bound is
attainable by the minimal polynomial itself.

