        [Turn 13 REASONING] (3850 chars)
        \[
x_{1},x_{2},\dots ,x_{2025}>0 ,\qquad 
M=\min _{(x_i)}\max\Bigl\{x_{1},\frac1{x_{1}}+x_{2},
\frac1{x_{2}}+x_{3},\dots ,\frac1{x_{2024}}+x_{2025},
\frac1{x_{2025}}+1\Bigr\}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Determination of the minimal possible value \(M\)

For a fixed number \(M>1\) we ask whether a sequence \((x_i)\) satisfying  

\[
\begin{cases}
x_{1}\le M,\\[2mm]
\displaystyle \frac1{x_{i}}+x_{i+1}\le M\qquad(i=1,\dots ,2024),\\[2mm]
\displaystyle \frac1{x_{2025}}+1\le M
\end{cases}
\tag{1}
\]

exists.  
If a solution exists, we can increase each \(x_i\) as far as (1) permits, because a larger
\(x_i\) makes the right‑hand side of the next inequality larger.
Hence the “most favourable’’ choice is obtained by taking all
inequalities as equalities except possibly the last one:

\[
\boxed{\;x_{1}=M,\qquad x_{i+1}=M-\frac1{x_{i}}\;(i=1,\dots ,2024)\;}
\tag{2}
\]

and finally we must have  

\[
\frac1{x_{2025}}+1=M\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
x_{2025}= \frac1{M-1}. \tag{3}
\]

Denote  

\[
f_{M}(t)=M-\frac1t .
\]

Iterating (2) gives  

\[
x_{2025}=f_{M}^{\,2024}(M).
\]

Consequently the smallest admissible \(M\) is the unique root \(>1\) of  

\[
f_{M}^{\,2024}(M)=\frac1{M-1}. \tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  An algebraic equation for \(M\)

Writing \(f_{M}(t)=\dfrac{Mt-1}{t}\) we recognise a Möbius transformation.
If  

\[
A(M)=\begin{pmatrix}M&-1\\[2mm]1&0\end{pmatrix},
\qquad 
f_{M}(t)=\frac{Mt-1}{t},
\]

then  

\[
f_{M}^{\,k}(t)=\frac{a_k t+b_k}{c_k t+d_k},
\qquad 
\begin{pmatrix}a_k&b_k\\c_k&d_k\end{pmatrix}=A(M)^{k}.
\]

For \(k=2024\) we obtain  

\[
f_{M}^{\,2024}(M)=\frac{a_{2024}M+b_{2024}}{c_{2024}M+d_{2024}} .
\]

Equation (4) is therefore equivalent to  

\[
\frac{a_{2024}M+b_{2024}}{c_{2024}M+d_{2024}}=\frac1{M-1},
\]

or after clearing denominators  

\[
\bigl(a_{2024}M+b_{2024}\bigr)(M-1)-\bigl(c_{2024}M+d_{2024}\bigr)=0.
\tag{5}
\]

The entries of \(A(M)^{k}\) are polynomials in \(M\) of degree \(k\);
hence (5) is a polynomial equation for \(M\) of degree  

\[
\deg (5)=2024+2=2026 .
\]

Thus the number \(M\) is an algebraic integer of degree \(2026\) over \(\mathbb{Q}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The quadratic that must divide \(F(x)\)

Let \(\alpha\) be a root of  

\[
x^{2}-Mx+1=0\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad M=\alpha+\frac1\alpha .
\tag{6}
\]

From (6) we see that \(\mathbb{Q}(\alpha)=\mathbb{Q}(M,\sqrt{M^{2}-4})\).
Because \(\deg_{\mathbb{Q}}M=2026\) and \(\sqrt{M^{2}-4}\) is not already in
\(\mathbb{Q}(M)\) (otherwise the quadratic would have rational coefficients),
the extension \(\mathbb{Q}(\alpha)/\mathbb{Q}\) has degree  

\[
\deg_{\mathbb{Q}}\alpha = 2\cdot 2026 = 4052 .
\]

Consequently the minimal polynomial of \(\alpha\) over \(\mathbb{Z}\) has degree
\(4052\); it is the resultant of the degree‑\(2026\) polynomial (5) and the
quadratic \(t^{2}-Mt+1\) with respect to \(M\).  Explicitly this resultant is  

\[
\Phi_{4052}(t)=t^{4052}-t^{4051}+t^{4050}-\dots -t+1,
\]

a cyclotomic‑type polynomial of degree \(4052\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Minimal degree of a polynomial divisible by \(x^{2}-Mx+1\)

A polynomial with integer coefficients that is divisible by
\(x^{2}-Mx+1\) must contain both roots \(\alpha\) and \(\frac1\alpha\); therefore
its degree is at least the degree of the minimal polynomial of \(\alpha\),
namely \(4052\).  The polynomial \(\Phi_{4052}(x)\) itself is monic,
has integer coefficients and is divisible by \(x^{2}-Mx+1\)
(because \(\alpha\) is a root), so the bound is attained.

