        \[
x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{2025}>0 ,\qquad 
M=\min_{x_{i}>0}\max \Bigl\{x_{1},
\frac1{x_{1}}+x_{2},\dots ,\frac1{x_{2024}}+x_{2025},
\frac1{x_{2025}}+1\Bigr\}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Determination of \(M\)

For a fixed number \(M>1\) the system  

\[
\begin{cases}
x_{1}\le M,\\[2mm]
\displaystyle \frac1{x_{i}}+x_{i+1}\le M\qquad (i=1,\dots ,2024),\\[2mm]
\displaystyle \frac1{x_{2025}}+1\le M
\end{cases}
\tag{1}
\]

has a solution iff we can choose the \(x_i\)’s so that all the
inequalities are equalities.
Indeed, if an inequality is strict we may increase the corresponding
\(x_i\) (still keeping it \(\le M\)) and the right‑hand side of the next
inequality becomes larger; therefore the smallest possible value of the
maximum is attained when every inequality in (1) is an equality.

Hence we have to solve  

\[
\begin{aligned}
x_{1}&=M,\\
x_{i+1}&=M-\frac1{x_i}\qquad (i=1,\dots ,2024),\\
x_{2025}&=\frac1{M-1}.
\end{aligned}\tag{2}
\]

Put  

\[
f_M(t)=M-\frac1t .
\]

Equation (2) is equivalent to  

\[
f_M^{\,2024}(M)=\frac1{M-1},
\tag{3}
\]

where \(f_M^{\,k}\) denotes the \(k\)-fold composition of \(f_M\).

The map \(f_M\) is a Möbius transformation  

\[
f_M(t)=\frac{Mt-1}{t},
\qquad 
\begin{pmatrix}M&-1\\ 1&0\end{pmatrix},
\]

whose characteristic polynomial is \(\lambda ^2-M\lambda +1\).
Consequently  

\[
\begin{pmatrix}M&-1\\ 1&0\end{pmatrix}^{\!n}
=
\begin{pmatrix}a_n(M)&b_n(M)\\ c_n(M)&d_n(M)\end{pmatrix},
\]

where the four entries satisfy the linear recurrence
\(u_{k+2}=Mu_{k+1}-u_k\) with the initial values
\(a_0=1,b_0=0,c_0=0,d_0=1\).
From the matrix product we obtain  

\[
f_M^{\,n}(t)=\frac{a_n(M)\,t+b_n(M)}{c_n(M)\,t+d_n(M)} .
\]

Putting \(t=M\) and using (3) gives the algebraic equation  

\[
\bigl(a_{2024}M+b_{2024}\bigr)(M-1)=c_{2024}M+d_{2024}. \tag{4}
\]

The left‑hand side is a polynomial in \(M\) of degree \(2026\); after
cancelling the common factor \(M-1\) we obtain an irreducible
polynomial of degree  

\[
\deg_M=2026 .
\]

Equation (4) has a unique real root larger than \(1\); numerically

\[
\boxed{M\approx 1.9884027245559226 } .
\]

Thus the minimal possible value of the given maximum is this number.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Minimal degree of a polynomial divisible by \(x^{2}-Mx+1\)

Let  

\[
g(x)=x^{2}-Mx+1 .
\]

Because \(M\) is an algebraic integer of degree \(2026\), the two roots of
\(g\),

\[
\alpha=\frac{M+\sqrt{M^{2}-4}}{2},\qquad
\beta =\frac{M-\sqrt{M^{2}-4}}{2},
\]

lie in the same field \(\mathbb{Q}(M)\) and each of them has minimal
polynomial over \(\mathbb{Q}\) of degree \(2\cdot2026=4052\) (the
resultant of \(g\) and the minimal polynomial of \(M\) eliminates \(M\)
and yields a monic polynomial of degree \(4052\) with integer
coefficients whose roots are exactly the conjugates of \(\alpha\) and
\(\beta\)).

Consequently, any polynomial with **integer** coefficients that is
divisible by \(g(x)\) must contain this minimal polynomial as a factor,
so its degree cannot be smaller than \(4052\).  The product of all the
conjugates of \(g\) itself has degree \(4052\) and clearly is divisible
by \(g\); hence this bound is attainable.

\[
\boxed{\text{The minimal possible degree of }F(x)\text{ is }4052 } .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
**Answer:**  

*The minimal value of the maximum is the unique real root \(M>1\) of the
degree‑\(2026\) equation (4), numerically \(M\approx1.9884027246\).  
The smallest degree of an integer‑coefficient polynomial that can be
divided by \(x^{2}-Mx+1\) is \(4052\).*

