        \[
M=\min_{x_i>0}\max\Bigl\{x_{1},\frac1{x_{1}}+x_{2},\dots ,
\frac1{x_{2024}}+x_{2025},\frac1{x_{2025}}+1\Bigr\}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Determining the minimal value \(M\)

If the minimum is attained, all the numbers in the set must be equal to the
same value \(t\); otherwise the largest one could be decreased.
Hence we have  

\[
\begin{cases}
x_{1}=t,\\[2mm]
\displaystyle x_{i+1}=t-\frac1{x_{i}}\qquad(i=1,\dots ,2024),\\[2mm]
\displaystyle \frac1{x_{2025}}+1=t .
\end{cases}
\tag{1}
\]

Define the linear‑fractional map  

\[
f_{t}(y)=t-\frac1y=\frac{ty-1}{y}.
\]

Writing \(f_{t}\) as a Möbius transformation,
\[
f_{t}(y)=\frac{ty-1}{y}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\begin{pmatrix}t&-1\\ 1&0\end{pmatrix}\! .
\]

Iterating \(f_{t}\) \(n\) times corresponds to the matrix power  

\[
\begin{pmatrix}t&-1\\ 1&0\end{pmatrix}^{\! n}
=
\begin{pmatrix}U_{n+1}&-U_{n}\\[1mm]U_{n}&-U_{n-1}\end{pmatrix},
\]

where the numbers \(U_{k}=U_{k}(t)\) satisfy  

\[
U_{0}=0,\qquad U_{1}=1,\qquad U_{k+1}=tU_{k}-U_{k-1}.
\tag{2}
\]

From (1) we need  

\[
f_{t}^{\,2024}(t)=\frac{U_{2025}t-U_{2024}}{U_{2024}t-U_{2023}}
   =\frac1{t-1}.
\tag{3}
\]

Using (2) and the identity \(U_{k}= \dfrac{\sin ( (k+1)\theta)}{\sin\theta}\)
with the substitution  

\[
t=2\cos\theta\qquad (0<\theta<\pi),
\]

equation (3) simplifies to  

\[
\sin\bigl((2025+1)\theta\bigr)=\sin\bigl((2025-1)\theta\bigr),
\]

which gives  

\[
\theta=\frac{\pi}{2\cdot2025+3}=\frac{\pi}{4053}.
\]

Consequently  

\[
\boxed{M=2\cos\frac{\pi}{4053}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The quadratic that must divide an integer‑coefficient polynomial

The quadratic with this \(M\) is  

\[
x^{2}-Mx+1
   =x^{2}-2\cos\frac{\pi}{4053}\,x+1
   =(x-e^{i\pi/4053})(x-e^{-i\pi/4053}).
\]

Its roots are  

\[
\zeta=e^{i\pi/4053}=e^{2\pi i/8106},
\qquad \zeta^{-1}=e^{-2\pi i/8106},
\]

i.e. a primitive \(8106\)-th root of unity and its inverse.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Minimal degree of an integer‑coefficient polynomial containing this factor

If a polynomial \(F(x)\in\mathbb Z[x]\) is divisible by the quadratic,
then \(F\) must have \(\zeta\) as a root.  
The minimal polynomial of a primitive \(8106\)-th root of unity over
\(\mathbb Q\) is the cyclotomic polynomial \(\Phi_{8106}(x)\); it is
irreducible in \(\mathbb Z[x]\).  
Therefore any integer‑coefficient polynomial having \(\zeta\) as a root
must be a multiple of \(\Phi_{8106}(x)\).

Hence the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is the degree of
\(\Phi_{8106}\), namely Euler’s totient of \(8106\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
8106 &=2\cdot3\cdot7\cdot193,\\
\varphi(8106)&=8106\Bigl(1-\frac12\Bigr)
               \Bigl(1-\frac13\Bigr)
               \Bigl(1-\frac17\Bigr)
               \Bigl(1-\frac1{193}\Bigr)=2304.
\end{aligned}
\]

