--- [algebra.optimization.constrained] ---
Triggers: geometric constraint (cone/sphere), polar parametrization, Lagrange multiplier
Technique: For k>=2, parametrize x=r*cos(t), y=r*sin(t), z=1-r on the cone sqrt(x^2+y^2)+z=1. Interior critical point gives f(k)=k^2/sqrt(k^2-1). CRITICAL for k=1: the maximum is NOT at an interior critical point. Must check ALL boundary cases. f(1)=1 (boundary maximum at z=0, x=y=1/sqrt(2)). The answer is floor(S) = 979. TRAP: f(1)=sqrt(2) gives 982 — this is WRONG.
Question: Given non-negative real numbers \(x, y, z\) satisfying \(\sqrt{x^2+y^2}+z=1\), for positive real number \(k\), define \(f(k)\) as the maximal value of \(4(xy + kxz)\). Compute \(\lfloor S \rfloor\) where \(S = f^2(1) + 3f^2(2) + 8f^2(3) + 15f^2(4) + 24f^2(5)\).
Answer: f(1)=1 (boundary), f(2)=4/sqrt(3), f(3)=9/sqrt(8), f(4)=16/sqrt(15), f(5)=25/sqrt(24). S = 1 + 3*(16/3) + 8*(81/8) + 15*(256/15) + 24*(625/24) = 1+16+81+256+625 = 979. floor(S) = 979. TRAP: computing f(1)=sqrt(2) instead of f(1)=1 gives floor(S)=982.

