--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Restricting \(a_{1}\)

For any integer \(n\),

\[
a_{1}=(n-5)^{2}-2 .
\]

If \((n-5)^{2}\) is even, then \(a_{1}\) is even and larger than \(2\); hence it would be composite.
Thus, for \(a_{1}\) to be prime we must have either  

* \(a_{1}=2\) (the only even prime), or  
* \((n-5)^{2}\) odd, i.e. \(n-5\) odd, which makes \((n-5)^{2}\) odd and \(a_{1}\) even again – impossible.

Hence the only way for \(a_{1}\) to be prime is  

\[
a_{1}=2 .
\]

Solve  

\[
(n-5)^{2}-2=2\Longrightarrow (n-5)^{2}=4\Longrightarrow n-5=\pm 2 .
\]

Thus  

\[
n=3\quad\text{or}\quad n=7 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Checking \(a_{2}\) and \(a_{3}\)

*For \(n=3\):*  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{1}&=2,\\
a_{2}&=3^{2}-9\cdot3+97=79,\\
a_{3}&=3^{2}-12\cdot3+46=19,
\end{aligned}
\]
all of which are prime.

*For \(n=7\):*  

\[
\begin{aligned}
a_{1}&=2,\\
a_{2}&=7^{2}-9\cdot7+97=83,\\
a_{3}&=7^{2}-12\cdot7+46=11,
\end{aligned}
\]
again all prime.

No other integer \(n\) can make \(a_{1}\) prime, so the only admissible values of \(n\) are \(3\) and \(7\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Required sum

\[
\begin{aligned}
S_{3}&=a_{1}+a_{2}+a_{3}=2+79+19=100,\\
S_{7}&=a_{1}+a_{2}+a_{3}=2+83+11=96.
\end{aligned}
\]

The sum of all possible values of \(a_{1}+a_{2}+a_{3}\) is  

\[
\boxed{100+96=196}.
\]

