        [Turn 1 REASONING] (4245 chars)
        Let  

\[
m\ge 100 ,\qquad m=100q+r,\qquad 0\le r\le 99 ,\qquad q,r\in \mathbb Z .
\]

For the \(99\) flavours \(F_{1},\dots ,F_{99}\) we ask whether we can put \(m\) candies
on a circle so that **every** block of \(100\) consecutive candies contains at least
one candy of each flavour.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A necessary condition

Fix a flavour \(F_i\) and look at the (cyclic) distances between two consecutive
candies of this flavour.  
If a distance were larger than \(100\), then the block of the \(100\) candies
lying completely inside this gap would contain no candy of flavour \(F_i\) – a
contradiction.  
Hence for every flavour the maximal distance between two consecutive
occurrences is at most \(100\).

If flavour \(F_i\) occurs \(a_i\) times, the \(a_i\) gaps cover the whole circle,
so the average gap is \(\dfrac{m}{a_i}\).  
Since the maximal gap is \(\le 100\),

\[
\frac{m}{a_i}\le 100\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad a_i\ge\Big\lceil\frac{m}{100}\Big\rceil .
\]

Thus each flavour must appear at least  

\[
t:=\Big\lceil\frac{m}{100}\Big\rceil
\]

times.  Adding the required appearances of the \(99\) flavours we obtain  

\[
99t\le m. \tag{1}
\]

If (1) fails, no arrangement is possible; consequently such an \(m\) is **not
colourful**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The condition is also sufficient

Assume (1) holds.  Put  

\[
t=\Big\lceil\frac{m}{100}\Big\rceil ,\qquad s=m-99t\;( \ge 0).
\]

First place exactly \(t\) candies of each flavour, arranging them as  

\[
\underbrace{F_{1},F_{2},\dots ,F_{99}}_{\text{first block}},
\underbrace{F_{1},F_{2},\dots ,F_{99}}_{\text{second block}},\dots ,
\underbrace{F_{1},F_{2},\dots ,F_{99}}_{\text{t‑th block}} .
\]

The length of this part is \(99t\).  
Inside it the distance between two consecutive equal flavours is exactly \(99\le
100\).

Now we still have \(s\) free positions (\(s=m-99t\)).  Put all of them as extra
copies of \(F_{1}\) at the end of the sequence.  
The distance between two consecutive \(F_{1}\)’s can only decrease, and the
distance that wraps round the circle (from the last \(F_{1}\) to the first
\(F_{1}\)) becomes

\[
m-\bigl((t-1)99+1+s\bigr)+1
   = (99t+s)-((t-1)99+s)+1 = 99\le 100 .
\]

Hence after this insertion **every** flavour occurs with gaps at most \(100\);
consequently every block of \(100\) consecutive candies contains all \(99\)
flavours.  Thus an arrangement exists, i.e. the given \(m\) is colourful.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Characterisation of colourful integers

Condition (1) can be rewritten in terms of the decomposition \(m=100q+r\).

*If \(r=0\) (i.e. \(m\) is a multiple of \(100\)) then \(t=\frac{m}{100}=q\) and
\(99t=99q\le 100q=m\); the condition holds.*

*If \(r>0\) then \(t=q+1\) and (1) becomes*

\[
99(q+1)\le 100q+r\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q+r\ge 99 .
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{\;m\text{ is colourful }\iff
\bigl(r=0\bigr)\ \text{or}\ \bigl(r>0\text{ and }q+r\ge 99\bigr).\;}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The non‑colourful integers are finite

The negation of the above condition is  

\[
r>0\ \text{and}\ q+r\le 98 .
\]

Since \(0\le r\le 99\) and \(q\ge 0\), only finitely many pairs \((q,r)\) satisfy
this, therefore only finitely many positive integers are not colourful.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  The largest non‑colourful integer

To maximise \(m=100q+r\) under the restriction \(r>0,\;q+r\le 98\) we take
\(q+r=98\) and make \(q\) as large as possible, i.e. \(q=97,\;r=1\).  
Then  

\[
m_{\max}=100\cdot 97+1 = 9701 .
\]

Indeed \(9701=99\cdot 97+98\) satisfies \(r=1,\;q+r=98\), so (1) fails:
\[
t=\Big\lceil\frac{9701}{100}\Big\rceil =98,\qquad 99t=9702>9701 .
\]
Hence \(9701\) cannot be coloured.  
Every integer larger than \(9701\) fulfills either \(r=0\) or \(q+r\ge 99\),
so by the construction of §2 it is colourful.

