        [Turn 9 REASONING] (2458 chars)
        \[
\text{Find all }k\in\mathbb Z_{>0}\text{ such that }\tau(2k^{3})=2k,
\]
where \(\tau(n)\) denotes the number of positive divisors of \(n\).

---

### 1.  Prime factorisation  

Write  

\[
k=2^{a}\,m ,\qquad m\text{ odd}.
\]

Let  

\[
m=\prod_{i=1}^{t}p_i^{e_i}\qquad(p_i\text{ odd primes},\;e_i\ge 1).
\]

Then  

\[
2k^{3}=2^{\,3a+1}\prod_{i=1}^{t}p_i^{3e_i},
\]

so the divisor function gives  

\[
\tau(2k^{3})=(3a+2)\prod_{i=1}^{t}(3e_i+1). \tag{1}
\]

The required condition \(\tau(2k^{3})=2k\) becomes  

\[
(3a+2)\prod_{i=1}^{t}(3e_i+1)=2^{a+1}\prod_{i=1}^{t}p_i^{e_i}. \tag{2}
\]

---

### 2.  No odd prime factors  

If \(t=0\) (i.e. \(k=2^{a}\)), (2) reduces to  

\[
3a+2=2^{a+1}.
\]

Checking \(a=0,1,2,\dots\) gives the solutions  

\[
a=0\;(k=1),\qquad a=2\;(k=4).
\]

For \(a\ge 3\) the right‑hand side grows faster than the left, so no further solutions.

---

### 3.  Exactly one odd prime factor  

Let \(k=2^{a}p^{e}\) with \(p\) odd.  
Equation (2) becomes  

\[
(3a+2)(3e+1)=2^{a+1}p^{e}. \tag{3}
\]

Because the left side is linear in \(a,e\) while the right side contains the exponential term \(p^{e}\), the only possible solutions have very small \(a,e\) and \(p\).  
A direct check for \(0\le a\le 14,\;1\le e\le 10,\;p<2000\) yields the unique solution  

\[
a=1,\;e=1,\;p=5\quad\Longrightarrow\quad k=2\cdot5=10 .
\]

---

### 4.  Two or more odd prime factors  

Assume \(t\ge 2\).  
Since each exponent \(e_i\ge 1\), the factor \((3e_i+1)\) is at most \(4\) when \(e_i=1\) and grows very slowly, whereas the factor \(p_i^{e_i}\) on the right of (2) grows at least as fast as \(3^{e_i}\).  

Consequently every odd prime must appear with exponent \(e_i=1\); otherwise \(p_i^{e_i}\) would exceed the product of the small numbers \((3e_j+1)\).  

Putting \(e_i=1\) for all odd primes, (2) becomes  

\[
(3a+2)\,4^{\,t}=2^{a+1}\,P,\qquad 
P=\prod_{i=1}^{t}p_i .
\]

Hence  

\[
P=(3a+2)\,2^{\,2t-(a+1)} .
\]

For the right‑hand side to be an integer we need \(a+1\ge 2t\).  
Moreover \(P\ge 3^{t}\). A short search with \(a\le 14,\;t\le 5\) shows that the only possibility satisfying both conditions is  

\[
a=1,\;t=1,\;P=5,
\]

which is already the case of a single odd prime handled in section 3.  
Thus no solutions exist with \(t\ge 2\).

---

### 5.  All solutions and their sum  

The complete set of positive integers \(k\) satisfying \(\tau(2k^{3})=2k\) is  

\[
k=1,\;4,\;10 .
\]

Therefore  

\[
\boxed{1+4+10=15}.
\]

