        The program computes the optimal expected number of cookies (i.e., the number of numbers remaining when the game ends) for a starting board of \(n\) copies of the number \(1\).

Running the code yields  

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
n & \text{optimal expected cookies} \\ \hline
1 & 1\\
2 & 2\\
3 & 2\\
4 & 3\\
5 & 3\\
6 & 4\\
7 & 4\\
8 & 5
\end{array}
\]

The pattern is clear: for each \(n\ge 2\) the optimal expected number of cookies is \(\displaystyle\left\lceil\frac{n}{2}\right\rceil\).

Hence, with the original board containing \(1997\) copies of \(1\),

\[
\boxed{\,\left\lceil\frac{1997}{2}\right\rceil = 998\,}
\]

