        The functional equation admits a whole one‑parameter family of solutions.  
For any real constant \(a\neq 1\) define  

\[
\boxed{P(x)=a\,x+\frac{a^{2}}{1-a}},\qquad 
\boxed{Q(x)=\frac{a}{1-a}\,x+\frac{a^{2}}{(1-a)^{2}}}.
\]

These functions satisfy  

\[
P\bigl(m+Q(n)\bigr)=nP(m)-mP(n)+Q(m)\qquad\forall m,n\in\mathbb R .
\]

When \(a=0\) this gives the trivial solution \(P\equiv0,\;Q\equiv0\).  
No other (non‑linear) solutions exist; the coefficient comparison forces any
solution to be linear, and the linear system yields exactly the family above.

Hence there are **infinitely many** (indeed, a continuum of) pairs \((P,Q)\) satisfying the condition.

