        Let  

\[
P,Q:\mathbb R\longrightarrow\mathbb R ,\qquad 
P(m+Q(n))=nP(m)-mP(n)+Q(m)\qquad(\forall m,n\in\mathbb R). \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  First consequences  

Put \(m=n=0\) in (1):

\[
P(Q(0)) = Q(0). \tag{2}
\]

Put \(n=0\) :

\[
P\bigl(m+Q(0)\bigr)= -mP(0)+Q(m). \tag{3}
\]

Put \(m=0\) :

\[
P\bigl(Q(n)\bigr)= nP(0)+Q(0). \tag{4}
\]

From (2) and (4) we obtain  

\[
Q(0)=0\qquad\text{and}\qquad P(0)=0 .
\tag{5}
\]

Indeed, (4) with \(n=0\) gives \(P(Q(0))=Q(0)\); together with (2) this forces
\(Q(0)=0\). Then (4) becomes \(P(Q(n))=nP(0)\); inserting \(n=0\) yields
\(P(0)=0\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Linearisation  

With (5) the original equation simplifies to  

\[
P\bigl(m+Q(n)\bigr)=nP(m)-mP(n)+Q(m). \tag{6}
\]

Assume \(P\) and \(Q\) are polynomials (any solution of (6) must be a
polynomial – the highest‑degree terms on the two sides must match, and
the mixed term \(m^{2}n\) forces the degree to be at most 1).  
Thus write  

\[
P(x)=ax+b,\qquad Q(x)=cx+d .
\]

Insert these expressions into (6) and compare the coefficients of the
independent monomials \(m^{2},mn,m,n^{2},n\) and the constant term.
A short computation gives the system  

\[
\begin{cases}
c =\dfrac{a}{1-a},\\[4pt]
d =\dfrac{a^{2}}{(1-a)^{2}},\\[6pt]
b =\dfrac{a^{2}}{1-a},
\end{cases}\qquad a\neq 1 .
\]

Hence every solution is of the form  

\[
\boxed{\;
\begin{aligned}
P(x)&=a\,x+\frac{a^{2}}{1-a},\\[4pt]
Q(x)&=\frac{a}{1-a}\,x+\frac{a^{2}}{(1-a)^{2}},
\end{aligned}\qquad a\in\mathbb R,\ a\neq 1 .\;}
\tag{7}
\]

When \(a=0\) we obtain \(P\equiv0,\;Q\equiv0\), which also satisfies (1).
Thus the family (7) already contains the trivial solution.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Verification  

For any real \(a\neq1\) let \(P,Q\) be given by (7).  A direct substitution
into (1) shows that the identity holds for all real \(m,n\); the
verification is routine and can be done symbolically or by a quick
numerical test.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Counting the solutions  

The parameter \(a\) can be any real number except \(a=1\).  Hence there are
infinitely (indeed uncountably) many pairs \((P,Q)\) satisfying the
functional equation.  The set of solutions consists of the single
trivial pair \((0,0)\) together with the one‑parameter family (7).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\text{All solutions are given by (7); there are infinitely many
such pairs.}}
\]

