---

### 1.  Looking for polynomial solutions  

Put  

\[
P(x)=ax+b,\qquad Q(x)=cx+d\qquad (a,b,c,d\in\mathbb R)
\]

and substitute in the functional equation:

\[
\begin{aligned}
P(m+Q(n)) &= a\bigl(m+cn+d\bigr)+b \\
          &= a m+ a c n + a d + b .
\end{aligned}
\]

The right‑hand side becomes  

\[
\begin{aligned}
nP(m)-mP(n)+Q(m)
   &= n(am+b)-m(an+b)+(cm+d)\\
   &= a n m +bn - a m n - bm + cm + d\\
   &= a c n + b m - b n + b + cm + d .
\end{aligned}
\]

Equating the two expressions and collecting the coefficients of the independent
variables \(m,n\) and the constant term gives the system  

\[
\begin{cases}
a+b-c =0,\\[2pt]
ac-b =0,\\[2pt]
ad+b-d =0 .
\end{cases}
\]

---

### 2.  Solving the coefficient system  

From \(ac=b\) we have \(b=ac\).  
Insert this into the first equation:

\[
a+ac-c=0\Longrightarrow a=c(1-a)\Longrightarrow 
c=\frac{a}{1-a}\qquad (a\neq 1).
\]

Hence  

\[
b=ac=\frac{a^{2}}{1-a},\qquad 
d=\frac{b}{1-a}= \frac{a^{2}}{(1-a)^{2}} .
\]

Thus for every real number \(a\neq 1\) we obtain a pair of linear functions  

\[
\boxed{\;
P_{a}(x)=a x+\frac{a^{2}}{1-a},\qquad 
Q_{a}(x)=\frac{a}{1-a}\,x+\frac{a^{2}}{(1-a)^{2}}\;}
\tag{1}
\]

which indeed satisfy the original equation (a direct substitution verifies it).

When \(a=0\) we obtain the trivial solution  

\[
P(x)\equiv0,\qquad Q(x)\equiv0 .
\]

No other possibilities arise: if \(P\) or \(Q\) had degree \(\ge 2\), the term
containing \(m^{2}n\) (or \(mn^{2}\)) in the expansion of the left‑hand side could
not be cancelled by the right‑hand side, forcing the leading coefficients to be
zero. Hence both functions must be linear, and the linear family (1) is the
complete set of solutions.

---

### 3.  Counting the solutions  

The parameter \(a\) can be any real number except \(a=1\).  
Therefore there are **infinitely many** (in fact, uncountably many) distinct
pairs \((P,Q)\) satisfying the functional equation.  

The trivial pair \((0,0)\) corresponds to the choice \(a=0\); all other choices
\(a\in\mathbb R\setminus\{1\}\) give non‑trivial solutions.

\[
\boxed{\text{There are infinitely many solutions.}}
\]

