### 1.  Linear Ansatz  

Because the equation is linear in both \(P\) and \(Q\) and the arguments appear only as
\(m\), \(n\) and \(m+Q(n)\), any polynomial solution must be of degree at most 1.
Hence we set  

\[
P(x)=ax+b,\qquad Q(x)=cx+d\qquad (a,b,c,d\in\mathbb R)
\]

and substitute.

---

### 2.  Substitution  

\[
\begin{aligned}
P\bigl(m+Q(n)\bigr)
   &=a\bigl(m+cn+d\bigr)+b
    =am+acn+ad+b,\\[2mm]
nP(m)-mP(n)+Q(m)
   &=n(am+b)-m(an+b)+(cm+d)\\
   &=nab-nbm-anm+mb+cm+d\\
   &=b(n-m)+cm+d .
\end{aligned}
\]

Equality for all \(m,n\) gives the system  

\[
\begin{cases}
am = -bm+cm &\Longrightarrow a=-b+c,\\[2mm]
acn = bn    &\Longrightarrow ac=b,\\[2mm]
ad+b = d    &\Longrightarrow (a-1)d+b=0 .
\end{cases}
\]

---

### 3.  Solving the coefficients  

From \(ac=b\) we have \(b=ac\).  
Insert this in the first equation:

\[
a = -ac + c \;\Longrightarrow\; a = c(1-a).
\]

If \(a=1\) the left‑hand side would be \(1\) while the right‑hand side is \(0\);  
hence \(a\neq1\) and we can divide by \(1-a\):

\[
c=\frac{a}{1-a},\qquad 
b=ac=\frac{a^{2}}{1-a}.
\]

Finally, from \((a-1)d+b=0\),

\[
d=\frac{-b}{a-1}= \frac{a^{2}}{(1-a)^{2}} .
\]

Thus for every real parameter \(a\neq1\) we obtain

\[
\boxed{%
\begin{aligned}
P(x)&=ax+\frac{a^{2}}{1-a},\\[2mm]
Q(x)&=\frac{a}{1-a}\,x+\frac{a^{2}}{(1-a)^{2}} .
\end{aligned}}
\]

When \(a=0\) this gives \(P\equiv0,\;Q\equiv0\); the trivial solution is included
in the same family.

---

### 4.  No higher‑degree solutions  

Assume \(\deg P\ge2\) or \(\deg Q\ge2\).  
Looking at the term of highest total degree in the original equation,
the left‑hand side contains a term \( \text{(leading coeff of }P)\, m^{k}Q(n)^{\ell}\) with
\(k+\ell\ge3\), while the right‑hand side contains at most quadratic terms
\(nP(m)\) and \(-mP(n)\).  The only way the equality can hold for all \(m,n\) is that
the leading coefficients are zero, forcing \(\deg P\le1\) and \(\deg Q\le1\).
Hence the linear family above exhausts all solutions.

---

### 5.  Number of solutions  

The parameter \(a\) can be any real number except \(a=1\).  
Therefore there are **uncountably many** distinct pairs \((P,Q)\) satisfying the
functional equation (the whole one‑parameter family).  

\[
\boxed{\text{Infinitely many (uncountably many) solutions.}}
\]

