        [Turn 2 REASONING] (1357 chars)
        The vectors are  

\[
\overrightarrow{MF}=F-M,\qquad \overrightarrow{ME}=E-M,
\]

where \(E\) and \(F\) are opposite points on the unit circle, i.e. \(F=-E\) and \(|E|=|F|=1\).

\[
\begin{aligned}
\overrightarrow{MF}\cdot\overrightarrow{ME}
&=(F-M)\cdot(E-M)\\
&=F\cdot E-F\cdot M-M\cdot E+M\cdot M\\
&= -1-(F+E)\cdot M+|M|^{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Since \(F+E= -E+E =0\),

\[
\boxed{\overrightarrow{MF}\cdot\overrightarrow{ME}=|M|^{2}-1 } .
\]

Thus the value depends only on the distance of \(M\) from the centre \(O\).  
To minimise the dot product we must minimise \(|M|\) for a point \(M\) on the edges of the regular dodecagon.

For a regular \(n\)-gon inscribed in a unit circle, the distance from the centre to any side (the apothem) is  

\[
a = \cos\frac{\pi}{n}.
\]

With \(n=12\),

\[
|M|_{\min}=a=\cos\frac{\pi}{12}.
\]

Hence  

\[
S = \min\bigl(\overrightarrow{MF}\cdot\overrightarrow{ME}\bigr)
    = \cos^{2}\frac{\pi}{12}-1
    = -\sin^{2}\frac{\pi}{12}.
\]

Now  

\[
\sin\frac{\pi}{12}= \sin 15^{\circ}= \frac{\sqrt6-\sqrt2}{4},
\qquad
\sin^{2}\frac{\pi}{12}= \frac{2-\sqrt3}{4}.
\]

Therefore  

\[
-10000\,S = 10000\sin^{2}\frac{\pi}{12}
           = 10000\cdot\frac{2-\sqrt3}{4}
           = 2500\,(2-\sqrt3)
           = 5000-2500\sqrt3\approx 669.872981.
\]

Taking the ceiling,

\[
\boxed{\left\lceil -10000\,S\right\rceil = 670 }.
\]

