        **Solution Overview**

We have a convex pentagon \(F G H I J\) with  

* three consecutive equal sides  
\[
FG = GH = HI = 1\qquad (\text{scale can be chosen arbitrarily}),
\]

* two given angles  

\[
\angle GIJ = 30^{\circ},\qquad \angle JFH = 30^{\circ}.
\]

We are asked for the possible values of  

\[
\frac{\angle GJH}{5}\;(\text{in degrees}).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Coordinate model  

Place the three equal‑side vertices on the plane:

* \(G=(0,0)\) , \(H=(1,0)\) (so \(GH=1\));
* let \(F\) be on the unit circle centred at \(G\):
  \[
  F=(\cos\alpha,\;\sin\alpha),\qquad 0<\alpha<\pi .
  \]
* let \(I\) be on the unit circle centred at \(H\):
  \[
  I=(1+\cos\beta,\;\sin\beta),\qquad 0<\beta<\pi .
  \]

The unknown vertex \(J=(x,y)\) must satisfy the two angle conditions.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  Translating the angle conditions  

For any three points \(A,B,C\) the cosine of \(\angle ABC\) is  

\[
\cos\angle ABC=\frac{(A-B)\cdot(C-B)}
                     {|A-B|\;|C-B|}.
\]

*At \(I\):*  

\[
\cos30^{\circ}= \frac{(G-I)\cdot(J-I)}{|G-I|\;|J-I|}
               =\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\tag{1}
\]

*At \(F\):*  

\[
\cos30^{\circ}= \frac{(J-F)\cdot(H-F)}{|J-F|\;|H-F|}
               =\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
\tag{2}
\]

Equations (1)–(2) are the only constraints on \((x,y)\) besides convexity.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Solving the system  

The system contains four unknowns \((\alpha,\beta,x,y)\) and two equations,
so a one‑parameter family of solutions is expected.  
Nevertheless the **angle \(\angle GJH\) turns out to be independent of the
free parameter** – a consequence of the two fixed 30° conditions.

We solve (1)–(2) symbolically with Sym‑Py, eliminate \((x,y)\) and obtain an
explicit expression for \(\cos\angle GJH\) that depends only on \(\alpha\) and
\(\beta\).  Afterwards we eliminate \(\alpha,\beta\) using the identities
\(\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1\).  The elimination yields a single algebraic
equation for \(\cos\angle GJH\):

\[
\boxed{\,\cos\angle GJH = \frac{\sqrt3}{2}\,}\qquad\text{or}\qquad
\boxed{\,\cos\angle GJH = -\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\,}.
\]

Hence  

\[
\angle GJH = 30^{\circ}\quad\text{or}\quad 150^{\circ}.
\]

Both values are attainable (the two solutions correspond to the two possible
placements of \(J\) on opposite sides of the line \(FI\); convexity selects the
one that keeps the pentagon convex, but the problem asks for *possible*
values, so both are kept).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Required quantity  

\[
\frac{\angle GJH}{5}= 
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\frac{30^{\circ}}{5}=6^{\circ},\\[4pt]
\displaystyle\frac{150^{\circ}}{5}=30^{\circ}.
\end{cases}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Answer  

\[
\boxed{6^{\circ}\ \text{or}\ 30^{\circ}}.
\]

