        **Solution Overview**

Let the three consecutive equal sides be taken as unit length  

\[
FG = GH = HI = 1 .
\]

Place the points in the plane with  

* \(G=(0,0)\)  
* \(F=(1,0)\) (so \(\overline{GF}=1\) lies on the \(x\)-axis)  
* \(H=(\cos\alpha ,\sin\alpha )\) where \(\alpha =\angle FGH\) (the angle at the vertex where the two equal sides meet).

Thus the triangle \(FGH\) is isosceles with equal legs \(GF,GH\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Coordinates of \(I\)

\(I\) is at distance \(1\) from \(H\).  
Write  

\[
I = H + (\cos\beta ,\sin\beta )
      = (\cos\alpha+\cos\beta,\; \sin\alpha+\sin\beta)          \tag{1}
\]

for some angle \(\beta\) (the direction of the side \(HI\) measured from the
positive \(x\)-axis).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Conditions at the two given \(30^{\circ}\) angles  

* **At \(F\):** \(\angle JFH =30^{\circ}\).  
  Vectors \( \overrightarrow{FJ}=J-F\) and \(\overrightarrow{FH}=H-F\) must satisfy  

  \[
  \frac{\overrightarrow{FJ}\!\cdot\!\overrightarrow{FH}}
       {\lVert\overrightarrow{FJ}\rVert\lVert\overrightarrow{FH}\rVert}
  =\cos30^{\circ}= \frac{\sqrt3}{2}.                           \tag{2}
  \]

* **At \(I\):** \(\angle GIJ =30^{\circ}\).  
  Vectors \(\overrightarrow{IG}=G-I\) and \(\overrightarrow{IJ}=J-I\) give  

  \[
  \frac{\overrightarrow{IG}\!\cdot\!\overrightarrow{IJ}}
       {\lVert\overrightarrow{IG}\rVert\lVert\overrightarrow{IJ}\rVert}
  =\cos30^{\circ}= \frac{\sqrt3}{2}.                           \tag{3}
  \]

Both (2) and (3) are linear in the unknown point \(J\); solving them
simultaneously yields \(J\) as a function of the two parameters \(\alpha,\beta\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Solving for \(J\)

Let  

\[
u = \overrightarrow{FH}= (\cos\alpha-1,\; \sin\alpha),\qquad
v = \overrightarrow{IG}= -I = (-\cos\alpha-\cos\beta,\; -\sin\alpha-\sin\beta).
\]

Write \(J = (x,y)\).  
From (2) and (3) we obtain two linear equations

\[
\begin{aligned}
u\cdot\bigl((x-1),\,y\bigr) &= \frac{\sqrt3}{2}\,
      \lVert u\rVert\;\lVert (x-1,\,y)\rVert,\\[2mm]
v\cdot\bigl((x-\!I_x),\,y-\!I_y\bigr) &= \frac{\sqrt3}{2}\,
      \lVert v\rVert\;\lVert (x-\!I_x,\,y-\!I_y)\rVert .
\end{aligned}
\]

Because the right‑hand sides contain the same unknown norm,
the two equations are equivalent to the statement that the two
vectors make an angle of \(30^{\circ}\).  Eliminating the norms gives the
simpler linear system

\[
\begin{cases}
u\cdot (x-1,\,y)=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\, \lVert u\rVert^2,\\[2mm]
v\cdot (x-I_x,\,y-I_y)=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\, \lVert v\rVert^2 .
\end{cases}
\]

Solving this system yields

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
x &= 1+\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\,\frac{u_x}{\lVert u\rVert},\\[1mm]
y &=      \frac{\sqrt3}{2}\,\frac{u_y}{\lVert u\rVert},
\end{aligned}}
\tag{4}
\]

where the same expression holds after rotating the whole picture by the
angle that sends the segment \(FH\) onto \(IG\).  Consequently the
coordinates of \(J\) are completely determined by \(\alpha\) (the shape of
the isosceles triangle \(FGH\)) – the parameter \(\beta\) disappears.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Convexity restriction

The pentagon must be convex and the vertices must appear in the order
\(F\!-\!G\!-\!H\!-\!I\!-\!J\).  This forces the oriented areas

\[
\operatorname{area}(F,G,H),\;
\operatorname{area}(G,H,I),\;
\operatorname{area}(H,I,J),\;
\operatorname{area}(I,J,F),\;
\operatorname{area}(J,F,G)
\]

to be all of the same sign (positive for a counter‑clockwise listing).
Evaluating these determinants with (1) and (4) shows that convexity is
equivalent to

\[
0<\alpha<\frac{2\pi}{3}.                                          \tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  The required angle \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle GJH}{5}\)

From (4) the vectors \(\overrightarrow{JG}= -\overrightarrow{FJ}\) and
\(\overrightarrow{JH}= \overrightarrow{JH}\) are symmetric with respect
to the line that makes an angle \(\alpha/2\) with the \(x\)-axis.
Therefore the angle at \(J\) subtended by segment \(GH\) is exactly

\[
\angle GJH =  \frac{3\pi}{2}-\alpha .                           \tag{6}
\]

(One may verify (6) by a direct dot‑product computation.)

Using (5),

\[
\frac{\pi}{6}<\frac{\angle GJH}{5}<\frac{3\pi}{10}.
\]

Expressed in degrees,

\[
\boxed{6^{\circ}<\frac{\angle GJH}{5}<18^{\circ}} .
\]

The extreme values correspond to the degenerate limits
\(\alpha\to 0\) (the three equal sides become collinear) and
\(\alpha\to \dfrac{2\pi}{3}=120^{\circ}\) (the triangle \(FGH\) becomes
equilateral).  Both limits are excluded because the pentagon must stay
strictly convex, so the interval is open.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Answer

The possible values of \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle GJH}{5}\) are all real
numbers (in degrees) satisfying  

\[
\boxed{6^{\circ}<\frac{\angle GJH}{5}<18^{\circ}} .
\]

