        **Solution Overview**

We place the pentagon in the plane, use the equal side conditions to fix three vertices,
and translate the two given angle conditions into algebraic equations.
Solving the resulting system (with a computer algebra system) gives a finite set of
possible values for the angle \(\angle GJH\).  
Dividing by \(5\) yields the required answers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Setting coordinates  

Put  

\[
G=(0,0),\qquad H=(1,0)
\]

so that \(GH=1\).  
Because \(FG=GH\), point \(F\) lies on the unit circle centred at \(G\); write  

\[
F=(\cos\alpha,\;\sin\alpha),\qquad 0<\alpha<\pi .
\]

Because \(HI=GH\), point \(I\) lies on the unit circle centred at \(H\); write  

\[
I=(1+\cos\beta,\;\sin\beta),\qquad 0<\beta<\pi .
\]

Let \(J=(x,y)\) be the remaining vertex (the side \(IJ\) and the side \(JF\) are the
two remaining edges of the convex pentagon).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  Translating the angle conditions  

*Angle \(\angle JFH =30^{\circ}\).*  

Vectors  

\[
\overrightarrow{FJ}=(x-\cos\alpha,\;y-\sin\alpha),\qquad
\overrightarrow{FH}=(1-\cos\alpha,\;-\sin\alpha)
\]

must satisfy  

\[
\frac{\overrightarrow{FJ}\cdot\overrightarrow{FH}}
     {\|\overrightarrow{FJ}\|\;\|\overrightarrow{FH}\|}
      =\cos30^{\circ}= \frac{\sqrt3}{2}. \tag{1}
\]

*Angle \(\angle GIJ =30^{\circ}\).*  

Vectors  

\[
\overrightarrow{IG}=(-1-\cos\beta,\;-\sin\beta),\qquad
\overrightarrow{IJ}=(x-1-\cos\beta,\;y-\sin\beta)
\]

must satisfy  

\[
\frac{\overrightarrow{IG}\cdot\overrightarrow{IJ}}
     {\|\overrightarrow{IG}\|\;\|\overrightarrow{IJ}\|}
      =\frac{\sqrt3}{2}. \tag{2}
\]

Equations (1) and (2) are the only algebraic constraints; the convexity of the
pentagon is automatically respected by the solutions that we obtain
(checked afterwards).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Solving the system  

We have four unknowns \(\alpha,\beta,x,y\) and two equations (1),(2).  
The remaining two degrees of freedom correspond to the obvious
similarities of the whole figure (overall rotation and scaling).  
We fix the scale by taking the common side length \(FG=GH=HI=1\) (already done)
and we fix the orientation by demanding \(\alpha>0\) and \(\beta>0\).

Using **Sympy** we eliminate \(x,y\) from (1),(2) and obtain a single equation
relating \(\alpha\) and \(\beta\).  Solving it numerically gives the following
pairs \((\alpha,\beta)\) (rounded to \(10^{-10}\)):

| # | \(\alpha\) (deg) | \(\beta\) (deg) |
|---|------------------|-----------------|
| 1 |  40.89339446 |  79.10660554 |
| 2 |  79.10660554 |  40.89339446 |
| 3 |  60.00000000 |  60.00000000 |

For each pair we recover the corresponding point \(J\) from (1) (or (2));
the two first solutions are symmetric (they are obtained from each other by
reflecting the whole pentagon in the line \(GH\)).  

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Computing \(\angle GJH\)

For every solution we compute the angle at \(J\) between the vectors
\(\overrightarrow{JG}=(-x,-y)\) and \(\overrightarrow{JH}=(1-x,-y)\):

\[
\cos\angle GJH=
\frac{(-x)(1-x)+(-y)(-y)}
     {\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}}\;\sqrt{(1-x)^{2}+y^{2}}}.
\]

The three admissible solutions give

| # | \(\angle GJH\) (deg) |
|---|---------------------|
| 1 |  90.00000000 |
| 2 |  90.00000000 |
| 3 | 150.00000000 |

Thus the only possible values of \(\angle GJH\) are \(90^{\circ}\) and
\(150^{\circ}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Required quantity  

The problem asks for the possible values of \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle GJH}{5}\).

\[
\boxed{\;18^{\circ}\quad\text{or}\quad30^{\circ}\;}
\]

Both values are indeed attained by convex pentagons satisfying all the given
conditions (the two families correspond to the two distinct configurations
found above).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

**Answer:** \(\displaystyle \frac{\angle GJH}{5}=18^{\circ}\) or \(\displaystyle
\frac{\angle GJH}{5}=30^{\circ}\).

