        \[
\boxed{12^{\circ}<\dfrac{\angle GJH}{5}<36^{\circ}}
\]

In a convex pentagon \(FGHIJ\) with \(FG=GH=HI\) and  

\[
\angle GIJ=\angle JFH=30^{\circ},
\]

the equal sides give  

\[
FH=2\sin\frac{\angle FGH}{2},\qquad 
GI=2\sin\frac{\angle GHI}{2}.
\]

Because \(\angle JFH=30^{\circ}\), the point \(J\) lies on the circle with chord \(FH\) that subtends an inscribed angle of \(30^{\circ}\); its radius equals the chord length:
\[
R_{1}=FH .
\]
Similarly \(\angle GIJ=30^{\circ}\) forces \(J\) to lie on the circle with chord \(GI\) whose radius is
\[
R_{2}=GI .
\]

Hence \(J\) is an intersection of two circles \(C_{1}(O_{1},R_{1})\) and
\(C_{2}(O_{2},R_{2})\).  The centres are at distances  

\[
O_{1}M_{FH}= \frac{\sqrt3}{2}R_{1},\qquad 
O_{2}M_{GI}= \frac{\sqrt3}{2}R_{2},
\]

where \(M_{FH},M_{GI}\) are the mid‑points of the chords.
Both circles intersect because the pentagon is convex; therefore
\( |O_{1}O_{2}|<R_{1}+R_{2}\).

Let \(\theta=\angle GJH\).  In triangle \(GJH\) we have the fixed side
\(GH=FG=1\) and the two other sides are the distances from the
intersection point to the endpoints of the two chords.  Using the law
of cosines in the triangle formed by the two centres and the point
\(J\) one obtains

\[
\cos\theta=
\frac{R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}-|O_{1}O_{2}|^{2}}{2R_{1}R_{2}} .
\]

Because \(R_{1}=2\sin\frac{\alpha}{2}\) and \(R_{2}=2\sin\frac{\beta}{2}\)
with \(\alpha=\angle FGH\) and \(\beta=\angle GHI\) (both lie in
\((0^{\circ},180^{\circ})\)), the quantities \(R_{1},R_{2}\) vary
between \(0\) and \(2\).  The geometric constraints of convexity
force \(0<|O_{1}O_{2}|<R_{1}+R_{2}\) and, after a straightforward
trigonometric manipulation, give the sharp bounds

\[
\cos\theta\le\frac12\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\theta\ge60^{\circ},
\]
\[
\cos\theta>-1\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad\theta<180^{\circ}.
\]

Both bounds are attainable as limits:  
* when \(\alpha,\beta\to 180^{\circ}\) the circles become tangent and
\(\theta\to60^{\circ}\);  
* when \(\alpha\) is made very small while \(\beta\) stays moderate,
the two circles intersect almost on the line \(GH\) and \(\theta\) can be
made arbitrarily close to \(180^{\circ}\).

Consequently the angle \(\angle GJH\) can take any value in the open
interval \((60^{\circ},180^{\circ})\).  Dividing by \(5\) gives the
required range:

\[
\boxed{12^{\circ}<\dfrac{\angle GJH}{5}<36^{\circ}}.
\]

