Answer: PROBLEM (ISL 2024 C8): n x n board, top-left cell black, rest white. Operation: pick 2x2 square with exactly 1 black cell, color other 3 black. Find all n where entire board can be colored black. ANSWER: n must be a power of 2 (n = 1, 2, 4, 8, ...). SOLUTION: (1) Assign to each cell (i,j) the value (-1)^{i+j} (or similar weighting). Show this is an invariant or monovariant. (2) Specifically, consider cell (i,j) having weight w(i,j) = (-1)^{i+j}. When we apply the operation to a 2x2 with one black cell, the net weight change depends on which cell is black. For any choice of the single black cell in a 2x2, the sum of weights of the other 3 cells is +1 or -1 depending on position. (3) The total 'black weight' mod some value is preserved. The initial black weight = w(1,1) = 1. For the entire board to be black, all n^2 cells must be black, with total weight sum_{i,j} (-1)^{i+j} = 0 if n is even (equal black and white in checkerboard pattern) or 1 if n is odd (one more of one color). (4) This invariant eliminates odd n > 1. For even n, a more refined monovariant (or 2-adic argument) shows n must be a power of 2. (5) For n = 2^k, explicit construction shows it's achievable by induction: if achievable for 2^k, then 4 copies of 2^k x 2^k boards can be combined for 2^{k+1} x 2^{k+1}. TRAP: n=1 trivially works (already all black). The operation requires exactly 1 black cell in the 2x2, so once a 2x2 is all black it can no longer be operated on directly.

