        [Turn 9 REASONING] (2660 chars)
        \[
\text{Find }n\ge 0\text{ such that }n^{3}+4n^{2}+6n+4\mid 20^{\,n+1}.
\]

---

### 1.  Factor the divisor  

\[
n^{3}+4n^{2}+6n+4=(n+2)(n^{2}+2n+2)
\]

and  

\[
n^{2}+2n+2=(n+1)^{2}+1 .
\]

Hence  

\[
D_n:=(n+2)(n^{2}+2n+2)
\]

must divide \(20^{\,n+1}=2^{2(n+1)}5^{\,n+1}\).

Consequently every prime divisor of \(D_n\) can only be \(2\) or \(5\); otherwise the
divisibility would be impossible.

---

### 2.  Coprimality of the two factors  

\[
\gcd (n+2,\;n^{2}+2n+2)=\gcd (n+2,\; (n^{2}+2n+2)-(n+2)(n)=2)=2 .
\]

Thus the two factors are essentially coprime, sharing at most a single factor \(2\).
Hence at most one of them may contain the prime \(5\).

---

### 3.  Possibilities  

Let  

\[
a=n+2,\qquad b=n^{2}+2n+2 .
\]

Because \(\gcd(a,b)=2\), the only possibilities are  

* \(a=2^{\alpha}\), \(b=2^{\beta}5^{\gamma}\)   (the factor \(5\) can be only in \(b\)),  
* \(a=2^{\alpha}5^{\gamma}\), \(b=2^{\beta}\)   (the factor \(5\) can be only in \(a\)).

We examine each case.

---

#### Case 1 : \(a=2^{\alpha}\).

Then \(n=2^{\alpha}-2\) and  

\[
b=n^{2}+2n+2
   =\bigl(2^{\alpha}-2\bigr)^{2}+2\bigl(2^{\alpha}-2\bigr)+2
   =4^{\alpha}-2^{\alpha+1}+2 .
\]

We need \(b\) to have only the primes \(2,5\).  
Checking \(\alpha=0,1,2,\dots\) gives  

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\alpha & n & b \\ \hline
1 & 0 & 2=2^{1} \\
2 & 2 & 10=2\cdot5 \\
3 & 6 & 50=2\cdot5^{2}
\end{array}
\]

For \(\alpha\ge 4\) the number \(b\) acquires a prime factor different from \(2,5\)
(easily verified, e.g. by factorising).  
Thus this case yields  

\[
n=0,\;2,\;6 .
\]

---

#### Case 2 : \(b=2^{\beta}\).

Now \(b=n^{2}+2n+2\) is a power of \(2\).  
Scanning \(n\ge 0\) shows that the only solution is \(n=0\) (where \(b=2\)).  
For any \(n\ge1\) the value \(b\) is congruent to \(2\pmod 4\) and larger than \(2\),
hence it is not a pure power of \(2\).

Thus no new \(n\) arise from this case.

---

### 4.  Verification of the exponent condition  

For the three candidates we check that the powers of \(2\) and \(5\) in \(D_n\)
do not exceed those in \(20^{\,n+1}\):

| \(n\) | \(D_n\) | factorisation | exponent of \(2\) ≤ \(2(n+1)\) | exponent of \(5\) ≤ \(n+1\) |
|------|---------|---------------|-------------------------------|----------------------------|
| 0 | \(2\cdot2=4\) | \(2^{2}\) | \(2\le 2\) | – |
| 2 | \((4)(10)=40\) | \(2^{3}\,5\) | \(3\le 6\) | \(1\le 3\) |
| 6 | \((8)(50)=400\) | \(2^{4}\,5^{2}\) | \(4\le 14\) | \(2\le 7\) |

Hence each indeed divides \(20^{\,n+1}\).

---

### 5.  All solutions and their sum  

The only non‑negative integers satisfying the condition are  

\[
n=0,\;2,\;6 .
\]

Therefore  

\[
\boxed{0+2+6 = 8 }.
\]

