        [Turn 8 REASONING] (2226 chars)
        \[
\begin{aligned}
&x,y,z\ge 0,\qquad 
 (x^3-3x^2+3x)+(y^3-3y^2+3y)+(z^3-3z^2+3z)=4 .
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 1.  A convenient substitution  

\[
x^3-3x^2+3x=(x-1)^3+1 .
\]

Put  

\[
a=x-1,\qquad b=y-1,\qquad c=z-1 .
\]

Then \(a,b,c\ge -1\) and the condition becomes  

\[
a^{3}+b^{3}+c^{3}=1. \tag{1}
\]

The expression to be maximised is  

\[
\begin{aligned}
x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}-x-y-z
&=(a+1)^{2}+(b+1)^{2}+(c+1)^{2}-(a+1)-(b+1)-(c+1)\\
&=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+a+b+c . \tag{2}
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus we have to maximise  

\[
F(a,b,c)=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+a+b+c
\]

under (1) and \(a,b,c\ge-1\).

---

### 2.  A linear‑combination trick  

For any real \(\lambda>0\),

\[
F =\sum_{cyc}\bigl(a^{2}+a-\lambda a^{3}\bigr)+\lambda\underbrace{(a^{3}+b^{3}+c^{3})}_{=1}
      =\lambda+\sum_{cyc}g_{\lambda}(a),
\]

where  

\[
g_{\lambda}(t)=t^{2}+t-\lambda t^{3},\qquad t\ge-1 .
\]

If we can bound \(g_{\lambda}(t)\le M(\lambda)\) for all \(t\ge-1\),
then  

\[
F\le 3M(\lambda)+\lambda .\tag{3}
\]

---

### 3.  Maximising \(g_{\lambda}\) on \([-1,\infty)\)

\(g'_{\lambda}(t)=2t+1-3\lambda t^{2}\).
The stationary points satisfy  

\[
3\lambda t^{2}-2t-1=0\Longrightarrow 
t=\frac{1\pm\sqrt{1+3\lambda}}{3\lambda}.
\]

Both solutions are \(\ge-1\) for \(\lambda>0\); together with the endpoint
\(t=-1\) they give all candidates for the maximum.

A direct computation shows that the choice \(\lambda=1\) makes the
largest value of \(g_{\lambda}\) equal to \(1\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
g_{1}(-1)&=1,\\
g_{1}(1)&=1,\\
g_{1}\!\left(\frac{1\pm\sqrt{4}}{3}\right)&\le 1 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence  

\[
M(1)=1 .
\]

Putting \(\lambda=1\) into (3) yields  

\[
F\le 3\cdot 1+1 =4. \tag{4}
\]

---

### 4.  Attaining the bound  

Equality in (4) requires each term \(g_{1}(a),g_{1}(b),g_{1}(c)\) to be
\(1\).  From the calculation above this happens only for  

\[
a,b,c\in\{-1,\,1\}.
\]

Together with the cubic condition (1),

\[
(-1)^{3}+1^{3}+1^{3}=1,
\]

the only admissible triples are permutations of  

\[
(a,b,c)=(-1,1,1).
\]

Returning to the original variables,
\[
(x,y,z)=(0,2,2)\quad\text{and its permutations}.
\]

For these values  

\[
x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}-x-y-z = 4 .
\]

Thus the bound (4) is sharp.

---

\[
\boxed{\,4\,}
\]

