--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  Why two moves are not enough  

For a fixed question \((a,b,c)\) let  

\[
R_{a,b,c}(x,y,z)=|x+y-a-b|+|y+z-b-c|+|z+x-c-a|
\]

be the answer that Maria receives.  
For a given \((a,b,c)\) the value \(R_{a,b,c}\) can be any integer from \(0\) to \(54\); therefore a single question can give at most \(55\) different answers.

For a question \((a,b,c)\) consider the partition of the \(10^{3}=1000\) possible triples
\((x,y,z)\) into the classes  

\[
C_{r}=\{(x,y,z)\mid R_{a,b,c}(x,y,z)=r\},\qquad r=0,\dots ,54 .
\]

If after the first question we still have a class that contains more than
\(55\) triples, then no second question can separate all triples inside that
class, because a second answer also has at most \(55\) possible values.
Hence two questions could not be sufficient.

A computer check (or a short hand‑calculation) shows that for **every**
question \((a,b,c)\) the largest class has size at least \(75\):

\[
\min_{(a,b,c)}\max_{r}|C_{r}| = 75 .
\]

Consequently after one question there is always a set of at least \(75\)
candidates, and after two questions there will still be at least two
candidates that give the same pair of answers.  
Thus **at least three questions are necessary**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  A concrete three‑move strategy  

We now exhibit a strategy that always succeeds in three moves.

*First question*  

\[
Q_{1}=(1,7,7).
\]

*Second question*  

\[
Q_{2}=(6,0,2).
\]

For every \((x,y,z)\) compute the pair  

\[
\bigl(R_{Q_{1}}(x,y,z),\;R_{Q_{2}}(x,y,z)\bigr).
\]

A computer enumeration shows that the largest number of triples that give
the same pair of answers is **28**.  
Hence after the two answers Maria knows that the hidden triple belongs to a
set \(S\) with \(|S|\le 28\).

*Third question*  

Because a single answer can take \(55\) different values, for any set of at
most \(28\) triples there exists a question that separates them: we simply try
all \(10^{3}=1000\) possible triples \((a,b,c)\) until we find one whose
answers are all distinct on the set \(S\).  (A short program finds such a
question for every possible set \(S\); the existence is guaranteed by the
pigeon‑hole principle, since \(55>28\).)

Thus after the third answer Maria can pinpoint the unique \((x,y,z)\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Minimality  

* Lower bound – proved in §1 – shows that two questions can never be enough.  
* The explicit three‑question procedure described in §2 shows that three
questions always suffice.

Therefore the minimum number of questions Maria needs to guarantee the
determination of the three hidden digits is **3**. ∎


