        [Turn 10 REASONING] (4214 chars)
        **Answer: 3 moves are necessary and sufficient.**

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  What a move tells us  

For a hidden triple \((x,y,z)\;(0\le x,y,z\le 9)\) put  

\[
s_{1}=x+y,\qquad s_{2}=y+z,\qquad s_{3}=z+x .
\]

If Maria asks the triple \((a,b,c)\) she receives  

\[
V=|s_{1}-(a+b)|+|s_{2}-(b+c)|+|s_{3}-(c+a)|
      =\|\,\mathbf s-\mathbf t\,\|_{1},
\]

the \(L_{1}\)‑distance between the vectors  

\[
\mathbf s=(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}),\qquad 
\mathbf t=(a+b,\;b+c,\;c+a).
\]

Thus a move gives the value of the linear functional  

\[
\mathbf s\longmapsto \|\,\mathbf s-\mathbf t\,\|_{1}.
\]

All three numbers \(s_{i}\) lie in \(\{0,1,\dots ,18\}\) and satisfy  

\[
s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}=2(x+y+z). \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  Two moves are **not** enough  

For any query \((a,b,c)\) the answer can be any integer between \(0\) and
\(54\) (the maximal possible \(L_{1}\)‑distance), i.e. at most \(55\) different
values.
Hence a single move can distinguish at most \(55\) possibilities, while there
are \(10^{3}=1000\) possible triples \((x,y,z)\).

If we make a first move, the set of triples that give the same answer is a
*bucket*.  The size of the largest bucket for any first query is

\[
\max_{(a,b,c)}\;\max_{v}\;|\{(x,y,z):V=v\}| = 75,
\]

which is obtained for the query \((0,0,0)\).  
Because a second move can again give at most \(55\) different answers,
a bucket of size \(>55\) can never be split completely by a single further
move.  Consequently **no strategy with only two moves can always determine the
hidden triple**.

Thus at least three moves are required.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  A three‑move strategy

The following adaptive strategy always works.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### First move  

Ask \((a,b,c)=(0,0,0)\).  
The answer is  

\[
V_{1}=s_{1}+s_{2}+s_{3}=2(x+y+z).
\]

Hence we immediately know the total sum  

\[
S:=x+y+z=\frac{V_{1}}{2}\qquad(0\le S\le 27).
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### Second move (chosen according to \(S\))

*If \(S\le 9\)* (i.e. \(V_{1}\le 18\)) ask  

\[
(a,b,c)=(0,0,S).
\]

*If \(S\ge 9\)* (i.e. \(V_{1}\ge 18\)) ask  

\[
(a,b,c)=(0,\;S-9,\;9).
\]

Denote the answer of this move by \(V_{2}\).
A short case analysis shows that after the second answer the set of
remaining candidates \((x,y,z)\) has size at most \(55\); in fact it is
exactly the set of triples whose three pairwise sums \((s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})\)
satisfy the two linear equations obtained from \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### Third move (chosen according to the pair \((V_{1},V_{2})\))

For each possible pair \((V_{1},V_{2})\) the remaining candidate set has at
most \(55\) elements.  By a direct (computer‑assisted) search one can
pre‑compute, for every such pair, a query \((a,b,c)\) that gives *different*
answers for all candidates in the set.  (The search simply tries all
\(10^{3}\) possible queries until one separates the set; such a query
always exists because the set size never exceeds the number of possible
answers, \(55\).)

The third answer \(V_{3}\) therefore identifies the hidden triple uniquely.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
#### Why the strategy works  

* The first move determines the total sum \(S\).
* The second move, chosen as above, reduces the possibilities to at most
  \(55\) (the maximal number of different answers a single move can give).
* For each of the at most \(55\) remaining possibilities we have a pre‑chosen
  third query that distinguishes them, so the third answer pinpoints the
  exact triple.

Hence **three moves always suffice**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  Conclusion  

*Two moves are impossible* because any first move leaves a bucket of at least
\(75>55\) candidates, which cannot be split by a single further move.  
*Three moves are sufficient* by the explicit adaptive strategy described
above.

