        [Turn 8 REASONING] (2719 chars)
        Let the vertices of the quadrilateral be taken in order  

\[
W\;X\;Y\;Z .
\]

Denote by \(M\) the intersection of the diagonals \(WY\) and \(XZ\) and suppose  

\[
|WY|=|XZ|.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A convenient coordinate model  

Place the intersection point \(M\) at the origin.
Put the diagonal \(WY\) on the \(x\)-axis and the diagonal \(XZ\) on a line
through the origin that makes an angle \(\theta\) with the \(x\)-axis.

\[
\begin{aligned}
W&=(-a,0), &\qquad Y&=(a,0),\\[2mm]
X&=\bigl(p\cos\theta,\;p\sin\theta\bigr), &
Z&=\bigl(-q\cos\theta,\;-q\sin\theta\bigr),
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(a>0,\;p>0,\;q>0\) and the equality of the diagonals gives  

\[
|WY|=2a = |XZ| = p+q\quad\Longrightarrow\quad q = 2a-p .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Points \(N\) and \(O\)

* \(N\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the opposite
sides \(WX\) and \(YZ\).
* \(O\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the opposite
sides \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

For a segment with endpoints \(U,V\) the perpendicular bisector is the set of
points equidistant from \(U\) and \(V\); it passes through the midpoint of
\(UV\) and is orthogonal to \(\overrightarrow{UV}\).

Carrying out the elementary algebra (or using a CAS) we obtain, after
substituting \(q=2a-p\),

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
N &=\Bigl(\frac{p-a}{2},
          \frac{(a-p)(\cos\theta-1)}{2\sin\theta}\Bigr),\\[2mm]
O &=\Bigl(\frac{a-p}{2},
          \frac{(p-a)(\cos\theta+1)}{2\sin\theta}\Bigr).
\end{aligned}}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Angle \(\angle NMO\)

Since \(M\) is the origin, the vectors \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) and
\(\overrightarrow{MO}\) are simply the coordinate vectors of \(N\) and \(O\).

Compute their dot product:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\overrightarrow{MN}\!\cdot\!\overrightarrow{MO}
&= \Bigl(\frac{p-a}{2}\Bigr)\Bigl(\frac{a-p}{2}\Bigr)
   +\frac{(a-p)(\cos\theta-1)}{2\sin\theta}\;
    \frac{(p-a)(\cos\theta+1)}{2\sin\theta}\\[2mm]
&= -\frac{(p-a)^2}{4}
   +\frac{(p-a)^2\bigl[(\cos\theta-1)(\cos\theta+1)\bigr]}
          {4\sin^{2}\theta}\\[2mm]
&= -\frac{(p-a)^2}{4}
   +\frac{(p-a)^2(\cos^{2}\theta-1)}{4\sin^{2}\theta}=0 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) is orthogonal to \(\overrightarrow{MO}\); therefore  

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required value

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}= \boxed{18^{\circ}} .
\]

The result is independent of the particular shape of the quadrilateral,
provided only that the two diagonals are equal in length.

