**Explanation**

Let the diagonals \(WY\) and \(XZ\) intersect at \(M\) and have the same length.
Put the intersection at the origin and write the vertices as  

\[
\begin{aligned}
W&=-a\mathbf u ,\qquad &Y&=(1-a)\mathbf u ,\\
X&=-b\mathbf v ,\qquad &Z&=(1-b)\mathbf v ,
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(\mathbf u,\mathbf v\) are unit vectors making an angle \(\theta\) \((0<\theta<\pi)\) with each other,
\(a,b\in(0,1)\) and the equality of the diagonals gives \(a+(1-a)=b+(1-b)=1\).

---

### 1.  Coordinates of \(N\)

\(N\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\).
A point \(P\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of a segment \(AB\) iff  

\[
|P-A|^{2}=|P-B|^{2}\Longleftrightarrow P\cdot (B-A)=\frac{|B|^{2}-|A|^{2}}{2}.
\]

Applying this to the two segments gives the linear system  

\[
\begin{cases}
N\cdot (X-W)=\dfrac{|X|^{2}-|W|^{2}}{2},\\[4pt]
N\cdot (Z-Y)=\dfrac{|Z|^{2}-|Y|^{2}}{2}.
\end{cases}
\]

Solving yields  

\[
N=\frac{b-a}{2}\Bigl(1,\;-\frac{\cos\theta+1}{\sin\theta}\Bigr).
\tag{1}
\]

---

### 2.  Coordinates of \(O\)

Similarly, \(O\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\).  
Solving the analogous system gives  

\[
O=\frac{1-a-b}{2}\Bigl(1,\;-\frac{\cos\theta-1}{\sin\theta}\Bigr).
\tag{2}
\]

---

### 3.  Angle \(\angle NMO\)

Since \(M\) is the origin, vectors \(\overrightarrow{MN}=N\) and \(\overrightarrow{MO}=O\) are
scalar multiples of the two vectors appearing in (1) and (2).  
Hence the angle \(\angle NMO\) equals the angle between  

\[
\mathbf v_{1}=\Bigl(1,\;-\frac{\cos\theta+1}{\sin\theta}\Bigr),\qquad
\mathbf v_{2}=\Bigl(1,\;-\frac{\cos\theta-1}{\sin\theta}\Bigr).
\]

Their dot product is  

\[
\mathbf v_{1}\!\cdot\!\mathbf v_{2}
=1+\frac{(\cos\theta+1)(\cos\theta-1)}{\sin^{2}\theta}
=1+\frac{\cos^{2}\theta-1}{1-\cos^{2}\theta}=0 .
\]

Thus \(\mathbf v_{1}\) and \(\mathbf v_{2}\) are perpendicular, so  

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

---

### 4.  Required value

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

Hence the answer is \(\boxed{18^{\circ}}\).

