and let the diagonals \(WY\) and \(XZ\) intersect at \(M\).
Put the intersection at the origin and denote the vectors of the vertices by  

\[
\begin{aligned}
W&=a\mathbf d ,\qquad &Y&=-b\mathbf d ,\\[2mm]
X&=c\mathbf e ,\qquad &Z&=-d\mathbf e ,
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(\mathbf d\) and \(\mathbf e\) are unit vectors making an angle
\(\theta\;(0<\theta<\pi)\) and \(a,b,c,d>0\).
Thus  

\[
WY\;:\; \mathbf d\quad\text{and}\quad XZ\;:\;\mathbf e .
\]

The hypothesis “the diagonals are equal’’ gives  

\[
|WY|=a+b=|XZ|=c+d\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad a+b=c+d. \tag{1}
