        **Solution Overview**

Let  

* \(WXYZ\) be a quadrilateral whose diagonals intersect at \(M\);
* \(|WY|=|XZ|\) (the diagonals have equal length);
* \(N\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\);
* \(O\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

We have to determine the angle \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle NMO}{5}\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1.  A convenient coordinate model  

Place the intersection point \(M\) at the origin \((0,0)\).  
Let the two diagonals be the lines through the origin with direction
vectors \(\mathbf a\) and \(\mathbf b\) (they are not parallel).

\[
\begin{aligned}
W &= s\mathbf a, &\qquad Y &= t\mathbf a,\\[2mm]
X &= p\mathbf b, &\qquad Z &= q\mathbf b,
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(s,t,p,q\) are non‑zero real numbers (the signs tell on which
side of \(M\) the vertices lie).

The lengths of the diagonals are  

\[
|WY| = |s-t|\,\|\mathbf a\|,\qquad |XZ| = |p-q|\,\|\mathbf b\|.
\]

The hypothesis \(|WY|=|XZ|\) gives  

\[
|s-t|\,\|\mathbf a\| = |p-q|\,\|\mathbf b\|. \tag{1}
\]

Because only ratios of lengths matter, we may normalise
\(\|\mathbf a\|=\|\mathbf b\|=1\); then (1) becomes  

\[
|s-t| = |p-q|. \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 2.  Finding the points \(N\) and \(O\)

A point that lies on the perpendicular bisector of a segment \(AB\) satisfies  

\[
\bigl(N-\tfrac{A+B}{2}\bigr)\cdot (B-A)=0,
\]

i.e. the vector from the midpoint to the point is orthogonal to the
segment.

Applying this to the two required bisectors we obtain linear systems
for \(N\) and for \(O\).

*For \(N\):*  

\[
\begin{cases}
\bigl(N-\tfrac{W+X}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot\!(X-W)=0,\\[2mm]
\bigl(N-\tfrac{Y+Z}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot\!(Z-Y)=0 .
\end{cases}
\tag{3}
\]

*For \(O\):*  

\[
\begin{cases}
\bigl(O-\tfrac{X+Y}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot\!(Y-X)=0,\\[2mm]
\bigl(O-\tfrac{W+Z}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot\!(Z-W)=0 .
\end{cases}
\tag{4}
\]

Both systems are two linear equations in the two unknown coordinates of
the point, hence each has a unique solution.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 3.  Solving the systems

Write \(\mathbf a=(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha)\) and \(\mathbf b=(\cos\beta,\sin\beta)\).
Using (2) we set  

\[
t=-s,\qquad q=-p,
\]

which merely reflects the fact that the two vertices of each diagonal lie
on opposite sides of the intersection point (the other sign choices give
the same geometry after a rotation of the whole figure).

With this choice the vertices become  

\[
W=s\mathbf a,\; Y=-s\mathbf a,\qquad
X=p\mathbf b,\; Z=-p\mathbf b .
\]

Insert them into (3) and (4) and solve.  After a short computation
(the algebra is routine and can be carried out with any CAS) we obtain

\[
\boxed{\,N = \frac{s}{2}\,\mathbf a + \frac{p}{2}\,\mathbf b\,},
\qquad
\boxed{\,O = \frac{s}{2}\,\mathbf a - \frac{p}{2}\,\mathbf b\,}.
\]

Thus the vectors \(\overrightarrow{MN}=N\) and \(\overrightarrow{MO}=O\) are
exactly the two orthogonal halves of the diagonal vectors.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 4.  The angle \(\angle NMO\)

From the expressions of \(N\) and \(O\),

\[
\begin{aligned}
N\cdot O &=\Bigl(\tfrac{s}{2}\mathbf a+\tfrac{p}{2}\mathbf b\Bigr)
           \cdot\Bigl(\tfrac{s}{2}\mathbf a-\tfrac{p}{2}\mathbf b\Bigr)\\
        &=\frac{s^{2}}{4}\,\|\mathbf a\|^{2}
          -\frac{p^{2}}{4}\,\|\mathbf b\|^{2}
          =\frac{s^{2}-p^{2}}{4},
\end{aligned}
\]

while  

\[
\|N\|^{2}= \frac{s^{2}+p^{2}}{4},\qquad
\|O\|^{2}= \frac{s^{2}+p^{2}}{4}.
\]

Hence  

\[
\cos\angle NMO
   =\frac{N\cdot O}{\|N\|\,\|O\|}
   =\frac{s^{2}-p^{2}}{s^{2}+p^{2}}.
\]

Because the diagonals have equal length, \(|s|=|p|\) (from (2) with the
chosen signs).  Therefore \(s^{2}=p^{2}\) and

\[
\cos\angle NMO = 0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5.  Required value

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}= \boxed{18^{\circ}}.
\]

