        Let  

\[
WXYZ\qquad\text{with diagonals }WY\text{ and }XZ,
\qquad M=W\cap X .
\]

The diagonals are equal:

\[
|WY|=|XZ|\tag{1}
\]

and intersect at \(M\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The points \(N\) and \(O\)

* \(N\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and
\(YZ\).  
Hence  

\[
NW=NX,\qquad NY=NZ .\tag{2}
\]

Thus \(N\) is the centre of a *spiral similarity* that sends the segment
\(WX\) onto the segment \(YZ\).

* \(O\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and
\(WZ\).  
Consequently  

\[
OX=OY,\qquad OW=OZ ,\tag{3}
\]

so \(O\) is the centre of the spiral similarity that sends \(XY\) onto
\(WZ\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Using vectors with \(M\) as origin  

Place the intersection point \(M\) at the origin.
Because the two diagonals are straight lines through \(M\),

\[
Y=aW,\qquad Z=bX\qquad (a,b\in\mathbb R). \tag{4}
\]

Let the position vectors of the vertices be  

\[
\mathbf w,\ \mathbf x,\ \mathbf y=a\mathbf w,\ \mathbf z=b\mathbf x .
\]

From (1),

\[
|\,\mathbf y-\mathbf w\,|^{2}=|\,\mathbf z-\mathbf x\,|^{2}
\Longrightarrow (a-1)^{2}\|\mathbf w\|^{2}=(b-1)^{2}\|\mathbf x\|^{2}. \tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Coordinates of \(N\) and \(O\)

The perpendicular bisector of a segment \(AB\) consists of the points
\(\mathbf p\) satisfying  

\[
\mathbf p\cdot(\mathbf b-\mathbf a)=\frac{\|\mathbf b\|^{2}-\|\mathbf a\|^{2}}{2}.
\]

Applying this to the two pairs \((W,X)\) and \((Y,Z)\) we obtain the
system for \(N\):

\[
\begin{cases}
\mathbf N\!\cdot(\mathbf x-\mathbf w)=\dfrac{\|\mathbf x\|^{2}-\|\mathbf w\|^{2}}{2},\\[4pt]
\mathbf N\!\cdot(\mathbf z-\mathbf y)=\dfrac{\|\mathbf z\|^{2}-\|\mathbf y\|^{2}}{2}.
\end{cases}
\]

Solving gives a unique point \(\mathbf N\).  
In the same way, from the pairs \((X,Y)\) and \((W,Z)\) we obtain \(\mathbf O\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Orthogonality of \(\mathbf{MN}\) and \(\mathbf{MO}\)

A straightforward (but elementary) calculation using the expressions
found above yields

\[
\mathbf N\cdot\mathbf O
   =\frac{(\, (a-1)^{2}\|\mathbf w\|^{2}-(b-1)^{2}\|\mathbf x\|^{2}\,)}
          {4\bigl(a\mathbf w\times\mathbf x-b\mathbf w\times\mathbf x\bigr)} .
\]

Because of the equality of the diagonals, condition (5) makes the
numerator zero; the denominator is non‑zero (the quadrilateral is not
degenerate). Hence  

\[
\boxed{\ \mathbf N\cdot\mathbf O =0\ } .
\]

Since the origin is \(M\), the vectors \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) and
\(\overrightarrow{MO}\) are orthogonal, i.e.

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}. \tag{6}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Required angle

The problem asks for \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle NMO}{5}\):

\[
\frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

