        Let  

* the diagonals be \(WY\) and \(XZ\) and let them intersect at \(M\);
* \(N\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the opposite sides
  \(WX\) and \(YZ\);
* \(O\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the opposite sides
  \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  What the points \(N\) and \(O\) mean  

The perpendicular bisector of a segment consists of all points that are
equidistant from its two endpoints.  
Hence  

\[
\begin{aligned}
NW &= NX, & NY &= NZ ,\\
OX &= OY, & OW &= OZ .
\end{aligned}
\tag{1}
\]

Thus \(N\) is the centre of a circle through \(W,X\) and also through
\(Y,Z\); similarly \(O\) is the centre of a circle through \(X,Y\) and
through \(W,Z\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  A useful auxiliary point  

Let \(P\) be the midpoint of the diagonal \(WY\) and \(Q\) the midpoint of
the diagonal \(XZ\).  
Because the diagonals are equal, \(|WY|=|XZ|\), the segments \(PQ\) and
\(MN\) are parallel and have the same length; the same holds for
\(PQ\) and \(MO\). Consequently

\[
MN = MO = PQ . \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Right angle at \(M\)

From (1) we have  

\[
NW = NX,\qquad NY = NZ \Longrightarrow N\text{ is the centre of the
circumcircle of }WXYZ\text{ taken in pairs }(W,X)\text{ and }(Y,Z).
\]

Hence \(N\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment that joins
the mid‑points \(P\) and \(Q\); i.e. \(NP\perp PQ\).  
In the same way \(O\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \(PQ\); thus
\(OP\perp PQ\).

Therefore the lines \(MN\) and \(MO\) are both perpendicular to the same
line \(PQ\); they are perpendicular to each other:

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}. \tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required angle  

The problem asks for \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle NMO}{5}\).

From (3),

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

