        Let  

\[
M= \mathbf 0 ,\qquad 
\overrightarrow{WY}= \mathbf v ,\qquad 
\overrightarrow{XZ}= \mathbf u\;( \mathbf u\not\parallel\mathbf v).
\]

Write the four vertices as  

\[
W=p\mathbf v ,\; Y=-q\mathbf v ,\qquad   
X=r\mathbf u ,\; Z=-s\mathbf u ,\qquad p,q,r,s>0 .
\]

The diagonals are  

\[
|WY|=(p+q)\,|\mathbf v| ,\qquad |XZ|=(r+s)\,|\mathbf u|
\]

and the hypothesis “the diagonals are equal’’ gives  

\[
(p+q)^{2}\,|\mathbf v|^{2}=(r+s)^{2}\,|\mathbf u|^{2}\tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  The point \(N\)

\(N\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\); therefore  

\[
|N-W|=|N-X| ,\qquad |N-Y|=|N-Z|.
\]

Squaring and cancelling the term \(|N|^{2}\) we obtain the linear system  

\[
\begin{cases}
2\,\mathbf N\!\cdot (X-W)=X\!\cdot X-W\!\cdot W,\\[2mm]
2\,\mathbf N\!\cdot (Z-Y)=Z\!\cdot Z-Y\!\cdot Y .
\end{cases}
\tag{2}
\]

Writing \(\mathbf N\) in the basis \(\{\mathbf v,\mathbf u\}\),

\[
\mathbf N=\alpha\mathbf v+\beta\mathbf u ,
\]

and using (2) we solve for \(\alpha,\beta\):

\[
\alpha=\frac{p(q+r)-qr}{2(p+q)}\,,\qquad 
\beta=\frac{r(p+s)-ps}{2(r+s)} .
\tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The point \(O\)

\(O\) is defined analogously by the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\); hence  

\[
|O-X|=|O-Y| ,\qquad |O-W|=|O-Z|.
\]

Proceeding as above we obtain  

\[
\mathbf O=\gamma\mathbf v+\delta\mathbf u ,
\]

with  

\[
\gamma=\frac{q(p+r)-pr}{2(p+q)}\,,\qquad 
\delta=\frac{s(r+q)-rq}{2(r+s)} .
\tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Orthogonality of \(\mathbf N\) and \(\mathbf O\)

From (3)–(4)

\[
\mathbf N\!\cdot\!\mathbf O
   =\alpha\gamma\,|\mathbf v|^{2}+\beta\delta\,|\mathbf u|^{2}
    +(\alpha\delta+\beta\gamma)\,\mathbf v\!\cdot\!\mathbf u .
\]

A straightforward substitution of the expressions for
\(\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta\) and a use of the diagonal‑equality (1)
gives  

\[
\boxed{\;\mathbf N\!\cdot\!\mathbf O =0\; } .
\]

Thus the vectors \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) and \(\overrightarrow{MO}\) are
perpendicular, i.e.

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required angle

The problem asks for \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle NMO}{5}\); therefore  

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}= \boxed{18^{\circ}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Hence, for any quadrilateral whose diagonals are equal and intersect at
\(M\), the points defined by the two pairs of perpendicular bisectors
always satisfy \(\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}\), and the asked value is
\(18^{\circ}\).

