        [Turn 10 REASONING] (3748 chars)
        Let  

* \(WXYZ\) be a quadrilateral whose diagonals \(WY\) and \(XZ\) intersect at \(M\);
* \(|WY|=|XZ|\);
* \(N\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the opposite sides \(WX\) and \(YZ\);
* \(O\) be the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of the opposite sides \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A convenient coordinate system  

Translate the figure so that the intersection point \(M\) becomes the origin.
Write the two diagonals as two lines through the origin:

\[
\begin{aligned}
WY &: \; \mathbf w = a\mathbf u ,\qquad \mathbf y =-b\mathbf u ,\\[2mm]
XZ &: \; \mathbf x = c\mathbf v ,\qquad \mathbf z =-d\mathbf v ,
\end{aligned}
\]

where  

* \(\mathbf u,\mathbf v\) are unit vectors (the directions of the two diagonals);
* \(a,b,c,d>0\) are the distances from \(M\) to the vertices on each diagonal.

Because the diagonals have the same length,

\[
|WY| = a+b = |XZ| = c+d\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad a+b=c+d. \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The points \(N\) and \(O\)

The perpendicular bisector of a segment \(PQ\) consists of the points \(X\) satisfying  

\[
\bigl(X-\tfrac{P+Q}{2}\bigr)\cdot (Q-P)=0 .
\]

Hence \(N\) is determined by  

\[
\begin{cases}
\bigl(N-\tfrac{\mathbf w+\mathbf x}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot(\mathbf x-\mathbf w)=0,\\[2mm]
\bigl(N-\tfrac{\mathbf y+\mathbf z}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot(\mathbf z-\mathbf y)=0,
\end{cases}
\]

and \(O\) by  

\[
\begin{cases}
\bigl(O-\tfrac{\mathbf x+\mathbf y}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot(\mathbf y-\mathbf x)=0,\\[2mm]
\bigl(O-\tfrac{\mathbf w+\mathbf z}{2}\bigr)\!\cdot(\mathbf z-\mathbf w)=0 .
\end{cases}
\]

Solving these linear systems (the equations are linear in the
coordinates of \(N\) and \(O\)) gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
N&=\Bigl(\frac{a^{2}d+b^{2}c-c^{2}d-cd^{2}}{2(ad-bc)},
          \frac{a^{2}b-a^{2}d\cos\theta+a b^{2}-a d^{2}
                -b^{2}c\cos\theta-bc^{2}+c^{2}d\cos\theta+cd^{2}\cos\theta}
               {2(ad-bc)\sin\theta}\Bigr),\\[2mm]
O&=\Bigl(\frac{a^{2}c+b^{2}d-c^{2}d-cd^{2}}{2(ac-bd)},
          \frac{-a^{2}b-a^{2}c\cos\theta-a b^{2}+a c^{2}
                -b^{2}d\cos\theta+b d^{2}+c^{2}d\cos\theta+cd^{2}\cos\theta}
               {2(ac-bd)\sin\theta}\Bigr),
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(\theta\) is the angle between the two diagonal directions,
\(\cos\theta=\mathbf u\!\cdot\!\mathbf v\) and \(\sin\theta>0\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Orthogonality of \(MN\) and \(MO\)

Since we placed \(M\) at the origin, the vectors \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) and
\(\overrightarrow{MO}\) are simply the position vectors of \(N\) and \(O\).
Their scalar product is

\[
\begin{aligned}
N\!\cdot\! O
&=
\frac{(a^{2}c+b^{2}d-c^{2}d-cd^{2})(a^{2}d+b^{2}c-c^{2}d-cd^{2})\sin^{2}\theta}
     {4(ad-bc)(ac-bd)}\\
&\qquad-
\frac{(a^{2}b+a^{2}c\cos\theta+a b^{2}-a c^{2}
        +b^{2}d\cos\theta-b d^{2}
        -c^{2}d\cos\theta-cd^{2}\cos\theta)
      (a^{2}b-a^{2}d\cos\theta+a b^{2}-a d^{2}
        -b^{2}c\cos\theta-bc^{2}+c^{2}d\cos\theta+cd^{2}\cos\theta)}
     {4(ad-bc)(ac-bd)} .
\end{aligned}
\]

Now use the equality of the diagonal lengths (1), i.e. \(d=a+b-c\).
Substituting \(d=a+b-c\) into the expression above makes the numerator
identically zero; consequently

\[
N\!\cdot\! O =0 .
\]

Thus the vectors \(MN\) and \(MO\) are perpendicular, i.e.

\[
\boxed{\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}} .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Required value

The problem asks for \(\displaystyle\frac{\angle NMO}{5}\):

\[
\frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

