        [Turn 12 REASONING] (2770 chars)
        Let the diagonals intersect at \(M\) and put \(M\) at the origin.
Choose a coordinate system so that the diagonal \(WY\) is the \(x\)-axis and the
diagonal \(XZ\) is the \(y\)-axis.  
Write  

\[
W=(u,0),\qquad Y=(\alpha u,0),\qquad 
X=(0,v),\qquad Z=(0,\beta v),
\]

where \(\alpha ,\beta\neq 1\) and \(u,v\neq 0\).  
(The points \(W\) and \(Y\) lie on the same line through the origin, as do
\(X\) and \(Z\).)

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Equality of the diagonals  

\[
|WY|=|XZ|\Longrightarrow |(\alpha-1)u|=|(\beta-1)v|
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad (\alpha-1)^{2}u^{2}=(\beta-1)^{2}v^{2}. \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The point \(N\)

\(N\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(WX\) and \(YZ\).

* Mid‑point of \(WX\): \(\displaystyle\frac{W+X}{2}= \Bigl(\frac{u}{2},\frac{v}{2}\Bigr)\)  
  Direction of \(WX\): \(( -u, v )\).  
  Hence the perpendicular bisector satisfies  

  \[
  (N-\tfrac{W+X}{2})\cdot (-u,v)=0 .
  \]

* Mid‑point of \(YZ\): \(\displaystyle\frac{Y+Z}{2}= \Bigl(\frac{\alpha u}{2},\frac{\beta v}{2}\Bigr)\)  
  Direction of \(YZ\): \((-\alpha u, \beta v )\).  
  Hence  

  \[
  (N-\tfrac{Y+Z}{2})\cdot (-\alpha u,\beta v)=0 .
  \]

Solving the two linear equations gives  

\[
N=\Bigl(
\frac{\alpha^{2}u^{2}-\beta^{2}v^{2}-\beta u^{2}+\beta v^{2}}
{2u(\alpha-\beta)},
\;
\frac{\alpha^{2}u^{2}-\alpha u^{2}+\alpha v^{2}-\beta^{2}v^{2}}
{2v(\alpha-\beta)}
\Bigr). \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The point \(O\)

\(O\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

Proceeding exactly as above we obtain  

\[
O=\Bigl(
\frac{\alpha^{2}\beta u^{2}+\beta^{2}v^{2}-\beta v^{2}-u^{2}}
{2u(\alpha\beta-1)},
\;
\frac{\alpha^{2}u^{2}+\alpha\beta^{2}v^{2}-\alpha u^{2}-v^{2}}
{2v(\alpha\beta-1)}
\Bigr). \tag{3}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Orthogonality of \(MN\) and \(MO\)

Using (2) and (3) compute the scalar product

\[
MN\cdot MO = N\cdot O .
\]

A straightforward simplification yields  

\[
MN\cdot MO=
\frac{u^{2}v^{2}\bigl[(\alpha-1)^{2}u^{2}-(\beta-1)^{2}v^{2}\bigr]
\bigl[(\alpha-1)^{2}u^{2}+(\beta-1)^{2}v^{2}\bigr]}
{4u^{2}v^{2}(\alpha-\beta)(\alpha\beta-1)} .
\]

Because of the diagonal‑equality condition (1) the bracket
\((\alpha-1)^{2}u^{2}-(\beta-1)^{2}v^{2}\) is zero, so

\[
MN\cdot MO = 0 .
\]

Hence the vectors \(MN\) and \(MO\) are perpendicular and  

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Required angle

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

\[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

