        [Turn 10 REASONING] (2544 chars)
        Let  

\[
W,\;X,\;Y,\;Z\in\mathbb R^{2},\qquad 
\overline{WY}=\overline{XZ},\qquad 
M=WY\cap XZ .
\]

Denote  

\[
N =\text{intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of }WX\text{ and }YZ,
\qquad 
O =\text{intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of }XY\text{ and }WZ .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Vector equations for the points  

Place the parallelogram \(WX\) on the \(x\)-axis:

\[
W=(-a,0),\qquad X=(a,0)\qquad (a>0).
\]

Write  

\[
Y=(u,v),\qquad Z=(p,q).
\]

The condition “the diagonals are equal’’ is  

\[
|Y-W|^{2}=|Z-X|^{2}\Longleftrightarrow (u+a)^{2}+v^{2}=(p-a)^{2}+q^{2}. \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  The point \(M\)

The lines \(WY\) and \(XZ\) are  

\[
W+t(Y-W),\qquad X+s(Z-X)\qquad (t,s\in\mathbb R).
\]

Solving  

\[
W+t(Y-W)=X+s(Z-X)
\]

gives the parameters  

\[
t=\frac{a(p-u)-qv}{(u+a)(p-a)+vq},\qquad 
s=\frac{a(p-u)+qv}{(u+a)(p-a)+vq},
\]

and consequently  

\[
M=W+t(Y-W)=X+s(Z-X). \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  The points \(N\) and \(O\)

*Perpendicular bisector of \(WX\).*  
Its equation is \(x=0\).  
Hence we may write \(N=(0,n)\).

*Perpendicular bisector of \(YZ\).*  
A point \(P\) lies on it iff \((P-\tfrac{Y+Z}{2})\cdot (Z-Y)=0\).  
With \(P=N\) we obtain  

\[
n=\frac{(u+p)(p-u)+(v+q)(q-v)}{2(q-v)} . \tag{3}
\]

Thus  

\[
N=\Bigl(0,\; \frac{(u+p)(p-u)+(v+q)(q-v)}{2(q-v)}\Bigr).
\]

For \(O\) we intersect the perpendicular bisectors of \(XY\) and \(WZ\).

\[
\begin{aligned}
(O-\tfrac{X+Y}{2})\cdot (Y-X)&=0,\\
(O-\tfrac{W+Z}{2})\cdot (Z-W)&=0 .
\end{aligned}
\]

Solving these two linear equations yields  

\[
O=\Bigl(\frac{a(p-u)+qv}{(u+a)(p-a)+vq},
        \frac{a(q-v)+(u+a)v}{(u+a)(p-a)+vq}\Bigr). \tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Orthogonality of \(\overrightarrow{MN}\) and \(\overrightarrow{MO}\)

From (2)–(4) we compute the scalar product

\[
(\,M-N\,)\cdot(\,M-O\,)
   =\frac{-(p-a)^{2}-q^{2}+(u+a)^{2}+v^{2}}
          {4\bigl((u+a)(p-a)+vq\bigr)^{2}} .
\]

The numerator is exactly the left–hand side of (1) with opposite sign;  
by the equal‑diagonal hypothesis (1) it vanishes. Hence  

\[
(\,M-N\,)\cdot(\,M-O\,)=0 .
\]

Therefore  

\[
\angle NMO = 90^{\circ}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Required angle

\[
\frac{\angle NMO}{5}= \frac{90^{\circ}}{5}=18^{\circ}.
\]

\[
\boxed{18^{\circ}}
\]

