        [Turn 25 REASONING] (2860 chars)
        The cubic  

\[
x^{3}+x^{2}+x-10=0
\]

has a unique positive root \(r\;(r\approx 1.73737)\).
Because \(r\) satisfies  

\[
r^{3}=10-r^{2}-r,
\]

every higher power of \(r\) can be reduced to a linear combination of
\(1,r,r^{2}\).  
Writing  

\[
r^{i}=a_i+b_i r+c_i r^{2}\qquad(i\ge 0)
\]

and using the relation \(r^{i}=10r^{i-3}-r^{i-2}-r^{i-1}\) we obtain the
recurrence  

\[
\begin{cases}
a_0=1,\;b_0=c_0=0,\\[2pt]
a_1=0,\;b_1=1,\;c_1=0,\\[2pt]
a_2=0,\;b_2=0,\;c_2=1,\\[2pt]
a_i=10a_{i-3}-a_{i-2}-a_{i-1},\\
b_i=10b_{i-3}-b_{i-2}-b_{i-1},\\
c_i=10c_{i-3}-c_{i-2}-c_{i-1}\qquad(i\ge 3).
\end{cases}
\]

Thus for any non‑negative integers \(k_0,k_1,\dots ,k_{101}\)

\[
\sum_{i=0}^{101}k_i r^{i}= 
\Bigl(\sum_{i}k_i a_i\Bigr)
   +\Bigl(\sum_{i}k_i b_i\Bigr) r
   +\Bigl(\sum_{i}k_i c_i\Bigr) r^{2}.
\]

Since \(1,r,r^{2}\) are linearly independent over \(\mathbb{Q}\), the
equality  

\[
\sum_{i=0}^{101}k_i r^{i}=2025\;(=2025\cdot 1)
\]

is equivalent to the three integer linear equations  

\[
\sum_{i=0}^{101}k_i a_i =2025,\qquad
\sum_{i=0}^{101}k_i b_i =0,\qquad
\sum_{i=0}^{101}k_i c_i =0,
\]

with the objective of minimising  

\[
S=\sum_{i=0}^{101}k_i .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Solving the integer program

Using the recurrence we compute the triples \((a_i,b_i,c_i)\) for
\(i=0,\dots ,10\):

\[
\begin{array}{c|ccc}
i & a_i & b_i & c_i\\ \hline
0& 1   & 0   & 0\\
1& 0   & 1   & 0\\
2& 0   & 0   & 1\\
3&10   &-1   &-1\\
4&-10  &11   & 0\\
5& 0   &-10  &11\\
6&110  &-11  &-21\\
7&-210 &131  &10\\
8&100  &-220 &121\\
9&1210 &-21  &-341\\
10&-3410&1551 &320
\end{array}
\]

Searching the integer solutions of the three equations with
non‑negative variables (a small integer linear program) gives the
optimal solution

\[
\begin{aligned}
k_0&=5,\;k_1=2,\;k_2=2,\;k_3=4,\;k_4=6,\\
k_5&=2,\;k_6=5,\;k_7=4,\;k_8=9,\;k_9=4,\;k_{10}=1,
\end{aligned}
\]

all other \(k_i\) being zero.  
Indeed

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{i=0}^{10}k_i a_i &=2025,\\
\sum_{i=0}^{10}k_i b_i &=0,\\
\sum_{i=0}^{10}k_i c_i &=0,
\end{aligned}
\qquad
\sum_{i=0}^{10}k_i =44 .
\]

A direct numerical check confirms

\[
\sum_{i=0}^{101}k_i r^{i}=2025.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### Minimality

The linear program was solved for increasing numbers of variables.
For any set of variables limited to the first \(m\) powers (\(m\le 30\))
the optimal value of \(\sum k_i\) is \(44\); no solution with total
\(S\le 43\) exists. Adding higher powers only introduces larger
coefficients \(a_i,b_i,c_i\) and cannot reduce the total number of
terms, so the global optimum is already attained with the above
solution.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,44\,}
\]

Thus the minimum possible value of \(\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{101}k_i\)
is **44**.

