        [Turn 10 REASONING] (3164 chars)
        For a positive integer \(n\) let  

\[
N(n)=\#\{(a,b)\in \mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid \gcd (a,b)=2023,\; \operatorname{lcm}(a,b)=n!\}.
\]

We have to find all \(n\) for which \(N(n)\le 4000\) and then sum those \(n\).

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  Counting the ordered pairs

Write  

\[
a=2023\;x,\qquad b=2023\;y ,
\]

so that \(\gcd(x,y)=1\).  
If \(\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)=n!\) then  

\[
2023\operatorname{lcm}(x,y)=n!\Longrightarrow \operatorname{lcm}(x,y)=\frac{n!}{2023}=M .
\]

Because \(\gcd(x,y)=1\), \(\operatorname{lcm}(x,y)=xy\); hence we need  

\[
x\,y=M,\qquad\gcd(x,y)=1 .
\]

Thus we have to split the prime‑power factorisation of \(M\) into two *coprime* factors.
For each distinct prime \(p\mid M\) the whole power \(p^{e_p}\) must go either to \(x\) or to \(y\); there are exactly two choices for each prime.  

If \(\omega(M)\) denotes the number of distinct prime divisors of \(M\),

\[
\boxed{N(n)=2^{\omega(M)}}\qquad\text{(ordered pairs)}.
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  When does \(M\) exist?

\(M=n!/2023\) is an integer iff \(2023\mid n!\).
Since  

\[
2023 = 7\cdot 17^{2},
\]

we need  

* at least one factor \(7\) in \(n!\)  \(\Longrightarrow\; n\ge 7\);
* at least two factors \(17\) in \(n!\)  \(\Longrightarrow\; \big\lfloor\frac{n}{17}\big\rfloor\ge 2\).

The second condition gives \(n\ge 34\).  
Hence  

\[
\begin{cases}
n\ge 34 &\Longrightarrow N(n)=2^{\omega\bigl(n!/2023\bigr)},\\[2mm]
n\le 33 &\Longrightarrow N(n)=0 .
\end{cases}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Computing \(\omega\bigl(n!/2023\bigr)\)

For a prime \(p\le n\),

\[
v_p(n!)=\sum_{k\ge 1}\Big\lfloor\frac{n}{p^{k}}\Big\rfloor .
\]

After dividing by \(2023\) we subtract one copy of \(7\) and two copies of \(17\):

\[
\tilde v_p(n!)=
\begin{cases}
v_7(n!)-1 & (p=7),\\[1mm]
v_{17}(n!)-2 & (p=17),\\[1mm]
v_p(n!) & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\]

A prime contributes to \(\omega\) iff \(\tilde v_p(n!)>0\).

Carrying this out for \(n\ge 34\) gives  

| \(n\) | \(\omega\bigl(n!/2023\bigr)\) | \(N(n)=2^{\omega}\) |
|------|------------------------------|---------------------|
| 34,35,36 | 10 | 1024 |
| 37,38,39,40 | 11 | 2048 |
| 41,42 | 12 | 4096 |
| \(n\ge 43\) | \(\ge 13\) | \(>4000\) |

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The condition \(N(n)\le 4000\)

* For \(n\le 33\) we have \(N(n)=0\le 4000\).  
* For \(34\le n\le 40\) we have \(N(n)=1024\) or \(2048\), both \(\le 4000\).  
* For \(n\ge 41\) we already have \(N(n)\ge 4096>4000\).

Hence the admissible \(n\) are  

\[
1,2,\dots ,33,34,35,36,37,38,39,40 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Summing the admissible \(n\)

\[
\sum_{n=1}^{33} n = \frac{33\cdot34}{2}=561,
\qquad
\sum_{n=34}^{40} n = \frac{(34+40)\cdot7}{2}=259.
\]

Total sum  

\[
\boxed{561+259 = 820}.
\]

Thus the sum of all positive integers \(n\) for which the number of ordered pairs \((a,b)\) with \(\gcd(a,b)=2023\) and \(\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)=n!\) does not exceed \(4000\) is **820**.

