        [Turn 7 REASONING] (3181 chars)
        The number of ordered pairs \((a,b)\) with  

\[
\gcd (a,b)=2023,\qquad \operatorname{lcm}(a,b)=n!
\]

is obtained as follows.

Write \(a=2023\;x,\;b=2023\;y\) with \(\gcd (x,y)=1\).
Then  

\[
\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)=2023\;xy=n!\quad\Longrightarrow\quad xy=\frac{n!}{2023}.
\]

Because \(\gcd(x,y)=1\), each prime factor of \(\dfrac{n!}{2023}\) must be placed
entirely in either \(x\) or \(y\).  
If \(\omega\bigl(\dfrac{n!}{2023}\bigr)\) denotes the number of distinct primes
dividing \(\dfrac{n!}{2023}\), the number of ordered coprime factorizations
\(xy=\dfrac{n!}{2023}\) is  

\[
2^{\omega\bigl(\frac{n!}{2023}\bigr)} .
\]

Hence the required number of ordered pairs \((a,b)\) equals  

\[
N(n)=2^{\omega\bigl(\frac{n!}{2023}\bigr)} .
\]

---

### 1.  When does a pair exist?

A pair exists only if \(2023\mid n!\).
Since \(2023=7\cdot 17^{2}\),

\[
v_{7}(n!)\ge 1,\qquad v_{17}(n!)\ge 2 .
\]

These inequalities hold exactly for \(n\ge 34\) (the smallest \(n\) with
\(v_{17}(n!)\ge 2\) is \(n=34\)).  
For \(n<34\) we have \(N(n)=0\).

---

### 2.  Computing \(\omega\bigl(\frac{n!}{2023}\bigr)\) for \(n\ge 34\)

Let \(\pi(n)\) be the number of primes \(\le n\).

* For a prime \(p\neq 7,17\) we have \(v_{p}(n!)\ge 1\); after division by
  \(2023\) the exponent remains positive, so such a prime always contributes
  to \(\omega\).

* For \(p=7\): after removing one factor of \(7\) (from \(2023\)) the exponent
  stays positive iff \(v_{7}(n!)\ge 2\), i.e. \(n\ge 14\).  
  For all \(n\ge 34\) this is true, so \(7\) contributes.

* For \(p=17\): after removing two factors of \(17\) the exponent stays positive
  iff \(v_{17}(n!)\ge 3\), i.e. \(n\ge 51\).

Hence  

\[
\omega\Bigl(\frac{n!}{2023}\Bigr)=
\begin{cases}
\pi(n)-1, & 34\le n\le 50,\\[2pt]
\pi(n),   & n\ge 51 .
\end{cases}
\]

---

### 3.  Condition \(N(n)\le 4000\)

Because \(N(n)=2^{\omega}\), the inequality \(N(n)\le 4000\) is equivalent to  

\[
\omega\le 11\qquad\bigl(2^{11}=2048,\;2^{12}=4096>4000\bigr).
\]

*For \(34\le n\le 50\):* we need \(\pi(n)-1\le 11\), i.e. \(\pi(n)\le 12\).
The smallest \(n\) with \(\pi(n)=12\) is \(n=37\); \(\pi(50)=15\) exceeds this,
so the admissible \(n\) are \(34,35,36,37,38,39,40\).

*For \(n\ge 51\):* we need \(\pi(n)\le 11\), which is impossible because
\(\pi(51)=15\). Hence no larger \(n\) satisfy the bound.

Thus for \(n\ge 34\) the admissible values are  

\[
n=34,35,36,37,38,39,40 .
\]

For each of these, \(N(n)=2^{10}=1024\) (for \(34\!-\!36\)) or
\(N(n)=2^{11}=2048\) (for \(37\!-\!40\)), both \(\le 4000\).

---

### 4.  Including the cases with zero pairs

If \(2023\nmid n!\) (i.e. \(n<34\)), then no ordered pair exists, so
\(N(n)=0\le 4000\).  
Consequently **all** positive integers \(n<34\) also satisfy the condition.

---

### 5.  Summing all admissible \(n\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{n=1}^{33} n &= \frac{33\cdot34}{2}=561,\\[2pt]
\sum_{n=34}^{40} n &= 34+35+36+37+38+39+40 = 259.
\end{aligned}
\]

Adding both parts,

\[
\boxed{561+259 = 820}.
\]

Hence the sum of all positive integers \(n\) for which the number of ordered
pairs \((a,b)\) with \(\gcd(a,b)=2023\) and \(\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)=n!\) does not
exceed \(4000\) is **820**.

