        \[
\begin{cases}
x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=3,\\[2mm]
xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}=3 .
\end{cases}
\]

---

### 1.  A Cauchy–Schwarz estimate  

Write the second sum as a scalar product  

\[
xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}= (x,\;y,\;z)\cdot (y^{3},\;z^{3},\;x^{3}).
\]

By Cauchy–Schwarz,

\[
\bigl(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}\bigr)\bigl(y^{6}+z^{6}+x^{6}\bigr)\ge
\bigl(xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}\bigr)^{2}.
\]

Using the first equation, \(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=3\), and the given value of the
right‑hand side, we obtain  

\[
3\bigl(x^{6}+y^{6}+z^{6}\bigr)\ge 9\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
x^{6}+y^{6}+z^{6}\ge 3. \tag{1}
\]

---

### 2.  Power‑mean inequality  

From the first equation the quadratic mean of \(|x|,|y|,|z|\) equals \(1\):
\[
\sqrt{\frac{x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}}{3}}=1 .
\]

The power‑mean inequality (with exponent \(6\)) gives  

\[
\sqrt[6]{\frac{x^{6}+y^{6}+z^{6}}{3}}\ge
\sqrt{\frac{x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}}{3}} =1,
\]

hence  

\[
x^{6}+y^{6}+z^{6}\ge 3. \tag{2}
\]

---

### 3.  Consequences of the two equalities  

Both (1) and (2) are equalities, therefore the equality cases of the
inequalities must hold.

* Equality in the power‑mean inequality forces  

  \[
  |x|=|y|=|z| .
  \]

* Equality in Cauchy–Schwarz requires the two vectors to be proportional:

  \[
  (x,\;y,\;z)=\lambda\,(y^{3},\;z^{3},\;x^{3})
  \quad\text{for some real }\lambda .
  \]

  Substituting \(|x|=|y|=|z|\) into this proportionality gives
  \(\lambda =\pm 1\) and consequently  

  \[
  x=y=z\quad\text{or}\quad x=y=z=-1 .
  \]

Thus each variable must be either \(1\) or \(-1\) and all three have the same
sign.

---

### 4.  Checking the possibilities  

The eight sign‑patterns \((\pm1,\pm1,\pm1)\) are tested in the original
system. Only

\[
(1,1,1)\qquad\text{and}\qquad (-1,-1,-1)
\]

satisfy both equations:

\[
\begin{aligned}
1^{2}+1^{2}+1^{2}=3,\qquad
1\cdot1^{3}+1\cdot1^{3}+1\cdot1^{3}=3,\\[2mm]
(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}=3,\qquad
(-1)(-1)^{3}+(-1)(-1)^{3}+(-1)(-1)^{3}=3 .
\end{aligned}
\]

No other sign pattern works.

---

\[
\boxed{\,2\,}
\]

Hence there are exactly **two** ordered triples of real numbers satisfying the
given system.

