        [Turn 28 REASONING] (1886 chars)
        \[
\begin{cases}
x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=3,\\[2mm]
xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}=3 .
\end{cases}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  An upper bound for \(xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}\)

For any real numbers \(a,b\),

\[
|ab^{3}|=|a|\,|b|^{3}\le\frac{a^{4}+3b^{4}}{4},
\]

because by AM–GM for the non‑negative numbers
\(a^{4},b^{4},b^{4},b^{4}\),

\[
\frac{a^{4}+b^{4}+b^{4}+b^{4}}{4}\ge
\sqrt[4]{a^{4}b^{4}b^{4}b^{4}}=|ab^{3}|.
\]

Applying this to the three terms we obtain  

\[
\begin{aligned}
|xy^{3}| &\le\frac{x^{4}+3y^{4}}{4},\\
|yz^{3}| &\le\frac{y^{4}+3z^{4}}{4},\\
|zx^{3}| &\le\frac{z^{4}+3x^{4}}{4}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Adding the three inequalities gives  

\[
|xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}|\le x^{4}+y^{4}+z^{4}. \tag{1}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Relating the fourth powers to the given quadratic sum

From the Power‑Mean (or Cauchy) inequality,

\[
\frac{x^{4}+y^{4}+z^{4}}{3}\ge\left(\frac{x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}}{3}\right)^{2}.
\]

Because \(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=3\),

\[
x^{4}+y^{4}+z^{4}\ge 3, \qquad\text{with equality iff }|x|=|y|=|z|=1. \tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Consequences for the system

From (1) and (2),

\[
3=xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}\le |xy^{3}+yz^{3}+zx^{3}|
\le x^{4}+y^{4}+z^{4}\le 3 .
\]

Hence all the inequalities must be equalities.  
Equality in (2) forces  

\[
|x|=|y|=|z|=1 .
\]

Equality in each AM–GM step used in (1) requires  

\[
x^{4}=y^{4}=z^{4}\quad\text{and}\quad xy^{3},\,yz^{3},\,zx^{3}\ge 0 .
\]

Thus the signs of \(x,y,z\) are the same.  
Consequently the only possibilities are  

\[
(x,y,z)=(1,1,1)\qquad\text{or}\qquad (x,y,z)=(-1,-1,-1).
\]

Both triples satisfy the original system, and no other real triple can do so.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
\[
\boxed{\,2\,}
\]

