        [Turn 7 REASONING] (1801 chars)
        \[
\begin{aligned}
f(z)&=pz^{3}+qz^{2}+rz+s,\\
f'(z)&=3pz^{2}+2qz+r,\\
f''(z)&=6pz+2q .
\end{aligned}
\]

The given equation is  

\[
f(z)f''(z)=(f'(z))^{2}\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
h(z):=f(z)f''(z)-(f'(z))^{2}=0 .
\]

---

### 1.  Express \(h(z)\) through the roots of \(f\)

Because the cubic \(f\) has three distinct real roots, write  

\[
f(z)=p\,(z-a)(z-b)(z-c),\qquad a<b<c,\;p\neq0 .
\]

Then  

\[
\begin{aligned}
f'(z)&=p\big[(z-b)(z-c)+(z-a)(z-c)+(z-a)(z-b)\big],\\
f''(z)&=p\big[2(z-a)+2(z-b)+2(z-c)\big]=2p\,(3z-a-b-c).
\end{aligned}
\]

A direct computation (or using a CAS) gives  

\[
\boxed{\,h(z)=-p^{2}\Big[(z-a)^{2}(z-b)^{2}+(z-a)^{2}(z-c)^{2}+(z-b)^{2}(z-c)^{2}\Big]\,}.
\]

---

### 2.  Sign of \(h(z)\) on \(\mathbb R\)

For any real \(z\),

* each factor \((z-a)^{2},(z-b)^{2},(z-c)^{2}\) is non‑negative,
* consequently each product \((z-a)^{2}(z-b)^{2}\), etc., is non‑negative,
* the sum of the three products is **strictly positive** because the three
  roots are distinct; it can be zero only if simultaneously  
  \((z-a)(z-b)=0,\;(z-a)(z-c)=0,\;(z-b)(z-c)=0\), which would force
  \(z=a=b=c\), impossible.

Hence  

\[
\sum_{i<j}(z-a_i)^{2}(z-a_j)^{2}>0\qquad\forall\,z\in\mathbb R .
\]

Since the factor \(-p^{2}\) is negative ( \(p\neq0\) ), we have  

\[
h(z)<0\qquad\forall\,z\in\mathbb R .
\]

Thus the equation \(h(z)=0\) has **no real solution**.

---

### 3.  Possible number of distinct real roots

The cubic condition guarantees \(p\neq0\) and three distinct real roots,
so the above analysis applies to every admissible choice of
\(p,q,r,s\). Consequently the equation  

\[
\bigl(pz^{3}+qz^{2}+rz+s\bigr)(6pz+2q)=\bigl(3pz^{2}+2qz+r\bigr)^{2}
\]

never possesses a real root.

\[
\boxed{\text{The only possible number of distinct real roots is }0.}
\]

