        [Turn 9 REASONING] (1687 chars)
        \[
f(z)=pz^{3}+qz^{2}+rz+s ,\qquad 
f'(z)=3pz^{2}+2qz+r ,\qquad 
f''(z)=6pz+2q .
\]

The given equation is  

\[
f(z)\,f''(z)=\bigl(f'(z)\bigr)^{2}\tag{1}
\]

and we are told that the cubic \(f(y)=0\) has three **distinct** real roots.
Write these roots as \(a<b<c\); then  

\[
f(z)=p(z-a)(z-b)(z-c),\qquad p\neq 0 .
\]

---

### 1.  Express \(f(z)f''(z)-(f'(z))^{2}\) in terms of the roots  

\[
\begin{aligned}
g(z)&:=f(z)f''(z)-(f'(z))^{2}\\
   &=p(z-a)(z-b)(z-c)\,\bigl[6p z+2q\bigr]
      -\bigl[3p z^{2}+2qz+r\bigr]^{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Using the factorisation \(f(z)=p(z-a)(z-b)(z-c)\) and expanding, a short
computation (or a symbolic one with *sympy*) gives  

\[
g(z)=-p^{2}\Bigl[(z-a)^{2}(z-b)^{2}
               +(z-a)^{2}(z-c)^{2}
               +(z-b)^{2}(z-c)^{2}\Bigr].\tag{2}
\]

Denote  

\[
h(z)= (z-a)^{2}(z-b)^{2}
     +(z-a)^{2}(z-c)^{2}
     +(z-b)^{2}(z-c)^{2}.
\]

Hence \(g(z)= -p^{2}h(z)\).

---

### 2.  Sign of \(h(z)\)

Each summand of \(h(z)\) is a product of two squares, therefore non‑negative
for every real \(z\).  
Because the three roots \(a,b,c\) are **distinct**, no real number can make
all three summands zero simultaneously:

* If \(z=a\) then the first two summands vanish, but the third becomes
  \((a-b)^{2}(a-c)^{2}>0\).
* The same happens for \(z=b\) or \(z=c\).

Consequently  

\[
h(z)>0\qquad\text{for all }z\in\mathbb R .
\]

Thus \(g(z)=0\) has **no real solution**.

---

### 3.  Number of distinct real roots of (1)

Equation (1) is equivalent to \(g(z)=0\).  
Since \(g(z)\) never vanishes on \(\mathbb R\), the equation possesses **no**
real root.

---

\[
\boxed{\text{The only possible number of distinct real roots is }0.}
\]

