        [Turn 10 REASONING] (2121 chars)
        \[
\begin{aligned}
f(z)&=pz^{3}+qz^{2}+rz+s,\\
f'(z)&=3pz^{2}+2qz+r,\\
f''(z)&=6pz+2q .
\end{aligned}
\]

The given equation is  

\[
f(z)\,f''(z)=\bigl(f'(z)\bigr)^{2}.
\tag{1}
\]

---

### 1.  Express \(f\) by its (real) roots  

Because the cubic \(py^{3}+qy^{2}+ry+s=0\) has three **distinct** real roots,
write  

\[
f(z)=p(z-a)(z-b)(z-c),\qquad a<b<c\in\mathbb R,\;p\neq0 .
\]

Then  

\[
\begin{aligned}
f'(z)&=p\bigl[(z-a)(z-b)+(z-a)(z-c)+(z-b)(z-c)\bigr],\\[2mm]
f''(z)&=p\bigl[2(z-a)+2(z-b)+2(z-c)\bigr]=2p\bigl(3z-(a+b+c)\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 2.  Compute the left‑hand side of (1)

\[
\begin{aligned}
f(z)f''(z)-\bigl(f'(z)\bigr)^{2}
&=p^{2}\Bigl[(z-a)(z-b)(z-c)\cdot 2\bigl(3z-(a+b+c)\bigr) \\
&\qquad-\bigl((z-a)(z-b)+(z-a)(z-c)+(z-b)(z-c)\bigr)^{2}\Bigr].
\end{aligned}
\]

A straightforward expansion (or a short symbolic computation) yields the
compact factorisation  

\[
\boxed{\,f(z)f''(z)-\bigl(f'(z)\bigr)^{2}
      =-p^{2}\Bigl[(z-a)^{2}(z-b)^{2}+(z-a)^{2}(z-c)^{2}+(z-b)^{2}(z-c)^{2}\Bigr]\,}.
\tag{2}
\]

---

### 3.  Analyse the sign of the right‑hand side  

For any real \(z\),

\[
(z-a)^{2}(z-b)^{2}\ge 0,\qquad
(z-a)^{2}(z-c)^{2}\ge 0,\qquad
(z-b)^{2}(z-c)^{2}\ge 0,
\]

and the three terms are **simultaneously zero** only when  
\(z\) equals at least two different numbers among \(a,b,c\), which is impossible
because the roots are distinct. Hence the sum in (2) is **strictly positive**
for every real \(z\).

Because \(-p^{2}<0\), we have  

\[
f(z)f''(z)-\bigl(f'(z)\bigr)^{2}<0\qquad\text{for all }z\in\mathbb R .
\]

Consequently the equation (1) has **no real solution**.

---

### 4.  Number of distinct real roots  

The quartic polynomial  

\[
g(z)=\bigl(pz^{3}+qz^{2}+rz+s\bigr)(6pz+2q)-\bigl(3pz^{2}+2qz+r\bigr)^{2}
\]

has degree 4 and, as shown above, never vanishes on \(\mathbb R\).
Therefore the equation \(g(z)=0\) possesses **zero** distinct real roots.

---

\[
\boxed{\,0\,}
\]

Thus the only possible number of distinct real roots of the given equation,
under the hypothesis that the cubic \(py^{3}+qy^{2}+ry+s=0\) has three
distinct real roots, is **0**.

