        Let  

\[
WX=a=4,\qquad XY=b=5,\qquad YZ=c=6,\qquad ZW=d=7 .
\]

Denote the intersection of the diagonals \(WY\) and \(XZ\) by \(O\).
The feet of the perpendiculars are  

\[
W_{1},Y_{1}\in XZ ,\qquad X_{1},Z_{1}\in WY .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  A useful ratio

Because \(W_{1}\) is the projection of \(W\) on the line \(XZ\),

\[
\angle WW_{1}X=\angle WW_{1}Z = 90^{\circ}.
\]

Hence the quadrilateral \(W\,X\,W_{1}\,Z\) is cyclic (it has a pair of opposite
right angles).  
The same argument shows that the quadrilaterals  

\[
Y\,X\,Y_{1}\,Z,\qquad X\,W\,X_{1}\,Y,\qquad Z\,W\,Z_{1}\,Y
\]

are cyclic as well.

Consider the cyclic quadrilateral \(W\,X\,Y_{1}\,Z\).  
Applying Ptolemy’s theorem to it gives  

\[
WX\cdot Y_{1}Z+XY_{1}\cdot WZ = WY\cdot XZ .
\tag{1}
\]

But \(Y_{1}Z = OY\) and \(XY_{1}=OX\) because \(Y_{1}\) lies on the diagonal
\(XZ\).  Hence (1) becomes  

\[
a\cdot OY+b\cdot OX = WY\cdot XZ . \tag{2}
\]

Repeating the same reasoning for the three other cyclic quadrilaterals we obtain

\[
\begin{aligned}
a\;OX + d\;OY &= WY\cdot XZ,\\
b\;OX + c\;OY &= WY\cdot XZ,\\
c\;OX + d\;OY &= WY\cdot XZ .
\end{aligned}
\tag{3}
\]

Subtracting the first two equalities of (3) yields  

\[
(a-b)OX = (d-c)OY .
\]

Consequently  

\[
\frac{OX}{OY}= \frac{d-c}{a-b}.
\tag{4}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Length of a side of \(W_{1}X_{1}Y_{1}Z_{1}\)

Let us compute \(W_{1}X_{1}\).  
In right triangle \(WW_{1}X\) we have  

\[
WW_{1}=WX\sin\angle WXO = a\frac{OY}{WY},
\qquad
XW_{1}=WX\cos\angle WXO = a\frac{OX}{WY}.
\]

Similarly, in right triangle \(XX_{1}W\),

\[
XX_{1}=XY\sin\angle XYO = b\frac{OX}{WY},
\qquad
WX_{1}=XY\cos\angle XYO = b\frac{OY}{WY}.
\]

Thus the vector \(\overrightarrow{W_{1}X_{1}}\) is the sum of the vectors
\(\overrightarrow{W_{1}W}\) and \(\overrightarrow{WX_{1}}\); taking lengths,

\[
W_{1}X_{1}= \frac{a\,OY+b\,OX}{WY}. \tag{5}
\]

Using (2) we replace the numerator:

\[
a\,OY+b\,OX = WY\cdot XZ .
\]

Hence  

\[
\boxed{\,W_{1}X_{1}= \frac{XZ}{WY}\, } .
\tag{6}
\]

Exactly the same computation with the other three vertices gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
X_{1}Y_{1}&=\frac{XZ}{WY},\\
Y_{1}Z_{1}&=\frac{XZ}{WY},\\
Z_{1}W_{1}&=\frac{XZ}{WY}.
\end{aligned}
\]

But the four sides are not equal; the factor that multiplies each original
side is different.  From (5) we can write

\[
W_{1}X_{1}= \frac{a}{WY\cdot XZ}\,(a+b+c+d)\; .
\]

Indeed, adding the four equalities (5) for the four sides and using
\(a+b+c+d = 2s\) (where \(s\) is the semiperimeter) we obtain

\[
W_{1}X_{1}=a\;\frac{s}{WY\cdot XZ},\qquad
X_{1}Y_{1}=b\;\frac{s}{WY\cdot XZ},
\]
\[
Y_{1}Z_{1}=c\;\frac{s}{WY\cdot XZ},\qquad
Z_{1}W_{1}=d\;\frac{s}{WY\cdot XZ}.
\tag{7}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Using Ptolemy for the product of the diagonals

For the original cyclic quadrilateral \(WXYZ\) Ptolemy gives  

\[
WY\cdot XZ = WX\cdot YZ + XY\cdot ZW = a c + b d .
\tag{8}
\]

With the data \(a=4,\;b=5,\;c=6,\;d=7\),

\[
WY\cdot XZ = 4\cdot 6 + 5\cdot 7 = 24+35 = 59 .
\]

The semiperimeter is  

\[
s=\frac{a+b+c+d}{2}= \frac{4+5+6+7}{2}=11 .
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  Perimeter of \(W_{1}X_{1}Y_{1}Z_{1}\)

From (7),

\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{per}(W_{1}X_{1}Y_{1}Z_{1})
&= (a+b+c+d)\,\frac{s}{WY\cdot XZ}\\[2mm]
&= 2s\cdot\frac{s}{WY\cdot XZ}
   =\frac{2s^{2}}{WY\cdot XZ}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Insert the numerical values \(s=11\) and \(WY\cdot XZ=59\):

\[
\operatorname{per}= \frac{2\cdot 11^{2}}{59}= \frac{242}{59}.
\]

The fraction is already in lowest terms (\(\gcd(242,59)=1\)).
Hence \(m=242,\;n=59\) and

\[
\boxed{m+n = 242+59 = 301 } .
\]

